// THE SUPPLIED INSTANCE ROOT, FOR A CONFINEMENT WITNESS THAT MUST NOT LIVE ON THE HOST IT DESCRIBES.
//
// A wet confinement witness proves something about a BOUNDARY by standing inside it and pushing: it installs a
// tree, writes under the confinement, and shows a write outside is refused. Both halves are WRITES, so the
// witness needs a root it is allowed to write — and until now the witnesses in this family got theirs from the
// production row: `roadmap_dashboard_instance.srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()`, whose `provision_parent_root` is
// `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent` = `/opt/gunbc-lab`. That gives a witness exactly two hosts, and neither
// one is a place a witness should be: on every host but srv1 the first `install` refuses (`install: cannot
// create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied` — nine of them on srv2, 2026-11-01), and on srv1 — which
// is LIVE, serving the lab — the claim passes by writing into the root the lab dispatches from. The row is
// filed as `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_confinement_witness_whose_only_writable_root_is_the_live_host`.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE SUPPLIES, AND WHY THIS SHAPE AND NOT ANOTHER.
//
//   THE ROOT IS AN MKTEMP LEAF UNDER A DECLARED SCRATCH PREFIX. `witness_scratch_prefix` is the only literal
//   any witness writes; the LEAF comes from `mktemp -d`, because a fixed scratch path is a rendezvous point
//   between two runs of the same witness on the same host — and a witness that inherits a tree it did not
//   create cannot tell its own write from a leftover, which is the difference between observing a confinement
//   breach and reading last week's file. The prefix is a `data` row per gunbc.design_argument::goal_safe_efficient_predictable, and the refusal arm
//   (`witness_supplied_root_is_refused`) is a PURE decision, so it is claimed in the hermetic corpus rather
//   than only under `--wet`: the guard that keeps a witness off the live root does not itself need a host.
//
//   THE PRINCIPAL IS THE HOST'S OWN, READ AND REQUIRED — NOT ONE THE WITNESS MINTS. The alternative was
//   `groupadd`/`useradd` in the fixture. It was rejected for three reasons that all point the same way:
//   (1) /etc/passwd and /etc/group are durable host state that no scratch prefix contains, so a witness that
//   mints an account has mutated the host outside the one tree it owns, with no seal and no rollback — the same
//   defect this work removes, moved from the filesystem to the account database; (2) it needs root, and a
//   confinement witness running as root answers the DAC question with root's privilege instead of with the
//   model's declared modes, so the very assertion it exists to make stops discriminating; (3) the production
//   names (`node`, gid 1003 as measured on srv1, and `gunbc-attempt` for the shared attempt group) are facts
//   about srv1's account database — srv2 has user `briansrls`, no group `node`, and neither `gunbc-worker` nor
//   `gunbc-attempt` — so nominating them measures the host's provisioning rather than the boundary. What is
//   kept is the pattern this family already uses for the same problem (`wet_group_token` in
//   `test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness`): ask the host what account is running,
//   REQUIRE an answer, and refuse when it does not answer. `witness_principal_from_texts` is the pure decision
//   — uid, gid and both names, refused when any is missing and refused when the uid is 0 — so the refusal is
//   claimed without spawning anything, and the uid/gid/mode legs stay exactly as strong: they are graded by the
//   host (`install -o -g -m`, `find -printf '%u %g %m'`, the sandbox's own EACCES) against the MODEL's modes,
//   and now against a principal the host actually SAYS instead of one inherited from srv1.
//
//   WHAT IS *NOT* SUPPLIED, WHICH IS THE PAIRING OBLIGATION (gunbc.design_argument::witness_one_interface). The instance is built from the
//   SAME production row the lab uses and the SAME production path helper it uses (`dashboard_instance_child`):
//   the deployment, the instance id, the layout, the serve function, the port, the admitted-revision source,
//   the publication branch, the worker identity, the toolchain and the memory cap all come from
//   `srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()` unchanged, so the only inputs that come from the witness are the root
//   prefix, the principal, the throwaway repository, and the transport (a witness is its own provision target;
//   `DashboardSshProvision { host: "srv1" }` would have a local test reach into the live host). The live row
//   keeps its one execution as a PURE derivation in
//   `test.claim.roadmap.dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_witness`, which asserts the shape the lab actually
//   gets — every derived path under the lab root, the owner, the group, the declared modes — without writing or
//   spawning. Deleting the real derivation makes THAT claim fail, which is what keeps supplying a root from
//   becoming, silently, deleting one.

// THE SCRATCH PREFIX EVERY WITNESS ROOT IS AN MKTEMP LEAF UNDER. A `data` row so the prefix is one admitted
// fact rather than a string scattered through fixtures; the unique leaf is always the host's, from `mktemp -d`.
data witness_scratch_prefix: NonEmptyStr = "/tmp" as NonEmptyStr

// THE LEAF TEMPLATE `mktemp -d` IS HANDED. The XXXXXX is mktemp's own token, not a placeholder: mktemp refuses
// to create the directory unless the template ends in at least three of them, and a refused mktemp is the
// fixture's `Absent` arm — a witness with no root declines, it does not fall back to a fixed path.
data witness_scratch_leaf_template: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc-wet-witness-XXXXXX" as NonEmptyStr

// THE CLAIM'S OWN LEAF, AS THE HOST NAMED IT. `mktemp -d` chose it, so nobody writing a claim can guess
// or reuse it -- which is why it is the token a probe puts inside a systemd unit name: two runs of the
// same claim cannot name the same unit, and no production attempt key can look like this. It is NOT a
// fourth copy of a `path_basename` helper -- `gunbc.live_deploy.member_observe` `path_basename`,
// `gunbc.url_file_acquisition` `path_basename` and `gunbc.gitignore_emit` `path_basename` each carry one
// for path RENDERING, where an empty input or a trailing separator is a thing to tolerate; a witness leaf
// is a host-chosen name that MUST exist, so an unnameable leaf is returned as Absent and the claim that
// asked for it refuses.
//
// WHY THAT IS THE ANSWER AND NOT A NEW DUPLICATION (DESIGN 6b, on the carrier). The three production
// copies TOLERATE the empty answer -- they fold to "" and their callers read that as "no basename" --
// while this one must REFUSE, which is a different function wearing the same name. Folding the three
// into one shared row is a genuine 6b root generalization and is NOT taken here: they live under three
// modules this lane has no authority over, and their tolerance of "" is exactly what their own callers
// depend on. TRIGGER: a fourth production caller needing the same tolerance -- then the shared row
// belongs beside the path type and all four fold through it.
fn witness_claim_leaf(path: String) -> String? {
  // PURE `.dag`, NO HOST PROCESS. A process was the wrong instrument here for the reason review 91550
  // gave: on a host where `basename` is not on PATH, or where a different coreutils answers, a claim
  // would have refused for a fact about the HOST TOOL and not about the sandbox -- and every unit-name
  // derivation paid a process start. The `basename` rows in `extdeps.tools.gnu_coreutils` are deleted
  // with this change; their only consumer was this line, and an extdeps row whose sole consumer is
  // semantics that need no executable is a dependency admitted by nothing (DESIGN 3c).
  //
  // The fold deliberately matches `basename(1)`, which strips trailing slashes: `basename /a/b/` is `b`,
  // not "", so empty segments are skipped. The separator cannot survive the split, so there is no
  // "result still contains a slash" arm to write -- an arm restating what the split already guarantees is
  // a guard that guards nothing.
  let leaf = fold(split(s: path, delimiter: "/"), init: "", f: (acc, seg) => if seg == "" { acc } else { seg })
  // THE REFUSALS ARE THE POINT: an empty read, or the filesystem root itself (what `basename /` prints),
  // is not a leaf, and a claim root is a `mktemp -d` leaf with a name under it.
  if leaf == "" {
    Absent
  } else {
    Present { value: leaf }
  }
}

fn witness_scratch_template() -> String {
  join([witness_scratch_prefix as String, "/", witness_scratch_leaf_template as String], "")
}

// THE HOST ROUTE, IN ONE PLACE. Three supplied-root witnesses need to run an ArgvCommand on the
// host and read its answer, and a third local copy of these three bodies was the trigger that
// gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization names -- "the trigger is the second local implementation of one rule". They live here, beside the
// prefix and the principal commands they serve, so there is one route from a witness to `id -u`,
// `stat -c %a` and `mktemp -d`: an ArgvCommand goes in as an ArgvCommand, which is what makes a
// refusal here a real refusal and not a string comparison.
//
// STILL DUPLICATED, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT QUIET:
// test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness keeps its own `wet`, `wet_ok` and
// `wet_out`, and test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_builder_wet_witness::wet_out its own `wet_out`
// (whose body inlines the capture call). They are not migrated here because the spawn witness's entry is
// refused today by the `circular dependency detected` in the roadmap_dashboard_instance_apply closure, so
// an edit to it could not be verified by running anything -- migrating a claim file whose claims cannot
// execute is exactly how a repair gets asserted without being observed. THE DEFECT IS NOT #15790: that PR
// landed (1a28f2a1bf01) and, by its own stated control, did NOT clear the refusal. What does not clear is
// a resolver defect in which a `decl_ref` citation STRING counts as a module-graph edge; it is routed to
// sleek-owl-397 via stern-bat-401. TRIGGER for the migration: the spawn witness's WHOLE-ENTRY resolve
// working -- not a two-claim `--functions` selection of that entry resolving, which hides the cycle
// (measured in the evidence recorded on PR #15794).
fn wet(command: ArgvCommand) -> ProcessOutcome {
  run_shell_command_capture(command: command, transport: LocalExec)
}

fn wet_ok(command: ArgvCommand) -> Bool {
  match wet(command: command) {
    ProcessRefused { exit_code: _, stderr: _ } => false
    ProcessOutputPresent { text: _ } => true
    ProcessOutputAbsent => true
  }
}

// THE WORD-LIST SIBLINGS OF wet / wet_ok / wet_out. A host operation modeled as an argv TAIL (the
// `_argv` rows in the extdeps modules: systemctl_stop_user_argv and
// systemctl_reset_failed_user_argv) carries no argv[0]; the caller names the program, and shell.Exec.RunArgv is the seam the
// whole fleet already uses for that shape. They exist here rather than in extdeps.exec.command because
// sealing the same words into an ArgvCommand needs a caller roster that project does not grow.
fn wet_argv_ok(program: NonEmptyStr, arguments: List<String>) -> Bool {
  // THE OUTCOME, NOT AN EXCEPTION -- and the same shape as `wet_out` below, which maps a ProcessRefused
  // to "" rather than raising. shell.Exec.RunArgv RAISES when the program cannot be spawned, and a
  // raised claim returns through the top of the interpreter: no removal of its root, no `claim-root-gone`
  // readback, no `[witness]` line (measured at 22c8f47b -- the unsandboxed control left
  // /tmp/gunbc-sandbox-unsandboxed-control-... behind on a host with no systemctl, and the only reason
  // the batch still reported it red was the leftover listing in the run script). "The host could not run
  // this argv" is a NO, and every caller of these two reads a NO as a refusal already.
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(program: program as String, arguments: arguments)
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } => false
    ShellExited => read.exit_code == 0
  }
}

fn wet_argv_out(program: NonEmptyStr, arguments: List<String>) -> String {
  // Same rule for the read side: no process, no output. A caller that expects text refuses on the empty
  // string -- `witness_claim_leaf` does -- instead of the claim dying inside a helper.
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(program: program as String, arguments: arguments)
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } => ""
    ShellExited => trim(s: read.stdout)
  }
}

fn wet_out(command: ArgvCommand) -> String {
  match wet(command: command) {
    ProcessOutputPresent { text: text } => trim(s: text)
    ProcessOutputAbsent => ""
    ProcessRefused { exit_code: _, stderr: _ } => ""
  }
}

// THE PATH-VALUED CAPTURE: stdout's BYTES, with exactly one trailing output terminator removed.
// `wet_out` trims trailing WHITESPACE, which is the right reading for a word (a uid, a mode, a
// digest line) and a DEFECT for a path: the kernel admits every byte but `/` and NUL in a name
// (path_resolution(7)), so a directory legitimately named `<base> ` prints, from realpath(1) or
// `pwd` or `mktemp -d`, as `<base>` + space + newline -- and `trim` collapses that distinct name
// onto `<base>`. Every equality and every `is_under` on the captured text then compares about a
// path the host never printed, and a writer that appends uses the UNTRIMMED spelling: the guard
// and the write disagree by the whitespace trim erased, which is how a sibling named `<base> `
// shared `<base>`'s admissions. What a line-oriented tool guarantees is ONE terminating newline
// -- nothing more -- so exactly one is removed and no other byte is touched. A refused or absent
// process is the empty string here exactly as in `wet_out`: no answer is still no answer.
fn witness_strip_one_output_terminator(text: String) -> String {
  if !ends_with(s: text, suffix: "\n") {
    text
  } else {
    join(list_init(xs: split(s: text, delimiter: "\n")), "\n")
  }
}

fn wet_out_verbatim(command: ArgvCommand) -> String {
  match wet(command: command) {
    ProcessOutputPresent { text: text } => witness_strip_one_output_terminator(text: text)
    ProcessOutputAbsent => ""
    ProcessRefused { exit_code: _, stderr: _ } => ""
  }
}

// THE TYPED CAPTURE RESULT (round 10, fail-closed): a refusal is a VARIANT, never a string.
// A fabricated sentinel string would be exactly the value DESIGN forbids -- a stand-in where
// a typed answer belongs: guards can collide with it, `is_under` accepts two equal failures as
// ancestry, and a failed create capture can reach `rm -r` as a path. Comparison, write and
// cleanup run ONLY on `ObservedPath`; every refusal carries its cause, and callers branch on
// the variant rather than on a magic spelling.
type CaptureResult
  = ObservedPath { path: String }
  | CaptureRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// THE TYPED LISTING: zero entries is a POSITIVE observation (exit 0, and the tool printed no
// names), never a shape a refusal may wear. A count is credited only against `Listed`.
type ChildListing
  = Listed { names: List<String> }
  | ListingRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// THE HOST'S LOSSY DECODE LEAVES A TRACE, AND THE TRACE IS THE REFUSAL: the RunArgv drain
// renders bytes that are not valid UTF-8 as U+FFFD (`String::from_utf8_lossy`,
// src/v1/stage0/src/bounded_shell_host_drain.rs), so a capture CONTAINING U+FFFD is a name
// whose bytes the transport already substituted away -- unobservable, refused. The -z family
// terminates with NUL (realpath(1), ls(1)); U+FFFD is never part of a path a claim may use.
fn witness_capture_has_replacement(text: String) -> Bool {
  string_contains(s: text, pattern: "\u{FFFD}")
}

// ONE TRAILING NUL, STRIPPED AS THE TERMINATOR IT IS. The -z family terminates each answer
// with exactly one NUL and no newline; the host drain's trailing-whitespace trim cannot touch
// a NUL, so the bytes of a printed path arrive intact. The whole capture contract: ACCEPT
// exactly `<one non-empty piece><one NUL>` and observe the piece; every other shape -- no
// terminator, a doubled terminator, an embedded NUL, an empty piece, a replacement character,
// no output -- is `CaptureRefused` with a cause. Nothing here trims, strips, or coerces.
fn witness_decode_one_nul_terminated(text: String) -> CaptureResult {
  if witness_capture_has_replacement(text: text) {
    CaptureRefused { cause: "the capture contains U+FFFD: the host substituted the replacement character for bytes it could not decode, so the path's own bytes are lost" }
  } else {
    let parts = split(s: text, delimiter: "\u{0}")
    match list_head(xs: parts) {
      Present { value: first } =>
        if count(parts) == 2 && ends_with(s: text, suffix: "\u{0}") && first != "" {
          ObservedPath { path: first }
        } else {
          CaptureRefused { cause: "the capture is not one non-empty piece terminated by exactly one NUL" }
        }
      Absent => CaptureRefused { cause: "the capture has no piece at all" }
    }
  }
}

// THE PATH-VALUED CAPTURE. Same RunArgv route as `wet_out_verbatim`, for commands of the -z
// family: the answer's bytes are kept and only the one NUL terminator is removed. A refused or
// absent process is `CaptureRefused`, never an empty answer: `realpath -m` refusing an
// unrecognized option on old coreutils must not read as "resolved to nothing".
fn wet_out_zero(command: ArgvCommand) -> CaptureResult {
  match wet(command: command) {
    ProcessOutputPresent { text: text } => witness_decode_one_nul_terminated(text: text)
    ProcessOutputAbsent => CaptureRefused { cause: "the process produced no observable output" }
    ProcessRefused { exit_code: _, stderr: _ } => CaptureRefused { cause: "the process refused" }
  }
}

// UNWRAP ONLY AN OBSERVATION, and only where the caller has ALREADY branched on the variant
// (`capture_was_refused`): the string a claim body carries exists solely in the
// `ObservedPath` arm's world; a refusal yields the inert empty string that no rm, tee, mkdir
// or is_under comparison can mistake for a path -- and the typed conjunct has already turned
// the claim red before any write is reached.
fn observed_path(capture: CaptureResult) -> String {
  match capture {
    ObservedPath { path: path } => path
    CaptureRefused { cause: _ } => ""
  }
}

fn capture_was_refused(capture: CaptureResult) -> Bool {
  match capture {
    ObservedPath { path: _ } => false
    CaptureRefused { cause: _ } => true
  }
}

// POSITIVE EVIDENCE OF A CREATED DIRECTORY BEFORE ITS NAME IS USED. mktemp(1) fills each X of
// the template from [A-Za-z0-9] (mktemp(1), GNU coreutils), so a CREATED name can never end in
// whitespace and the host's trailing-whitespace trim has nothing to take -- that argument is a
// property of the template at each call site, which is why it lives beside this helper rather
// than being assumed of the transport. On top of it, the capture is CHECKED: it must carry no
// replacement character and must pass `witness_path_is_directory` -- the typed `stat -c %F`
// reading -- for the name to be observed at all. A refusal never reaches a caller's writes or
// cleanup: it is a `CaptureRefused` with a cause, and the claim branches on it.
fn wet_out_created_directory(command: ArgvCommand) -> CaptureResult {
  let captured = wet_out_verbatim(command: command)
  if captured == "" {
    CaptureRefused { cause: "the create printed nothing it could be held to" }
  } else if witness_capture_has_replacement(text: captured) {
    CaptureRefused { cause: "the create answer contains U+FFFD: the host substituted the replacement character for bytes it could not decode" }
  } else if !witness_path_is_directory(candidate: captured) {
    CaptureRefused { cause: "the captured create answer does not positively stat as a directory under that exact spelling" }
  } else {
    ObservedPath { path: captured }
  }
}

// ENTRIES AS A TYPED LISTING: `ls_child_names_command` prints each entry name terminated by
// one NUL (ls -A --zero; ls(1)). Decode ZERO OR MORE NUL-terminated entries; a failed listing,
// a refusal, a replacement character, a missing or doubled terminator, or an empty entry is
// `ListingRefused` -- NEVER a zero count, which is reserved for `Listed` with no names (exit 0
// and no output). The split is on NUL, so no name can forge a boundary the way a
// newline-in-name forged one through the old newline split (round 6 P2).
fn witness_decode_nul_entries(text: String) -> ChildListing {
  if witness_capture_has_replacement(text: text) {
    ListingRefused { cause: "the listing contains U+FFFD: the host substituted the replacement character for bytes it could not decode" }
  } else if text == "" {
    Listed { names: list_init(xs: []) }
  } else if !ends_with(s: text, suffix: "\u{0}") {
    ListingRefused { cause: "a listing entry was not NUL-terminated" }
  } else {
    let parts = split(s: text, delimiter: "\u{0}")
    if count(filter(parts, e => e == "")) == 1 {
      Listed { names: filter(parts, e => e != "") }
    } else {
      ListingRefused { cause: "the listing carries an empty entry or a doubled terminator" }
    }
  }
}

fn wet_out_child_names(path: String) -> ChildListing {
  match wet(command: ls_child_names_command(path: path)) {
    ProcessOutputPresent { text: text } => witness_decode_nul_entries(text: text)
    ProcessOutputAbsent => ListingRefused { cause: "the listing produced no observable output" }
    ProcessRefused { exit_code: _, stderr: _ } => ListingRefused { cause: "the listing process refused" }
  }
}

// THE ONE PROCESS THE FIXTURE NEEDS TO GET A ROOT. Callers run this with `run_shell_command_observe` and read
// the created path off stdout; an empty or absent stdout is `Absent`, which every claim turns into a refusal
// rather than a default.
// THE CREDENTIAL A SUPPLIED UNIT IS HANDED, UNDER THE WITNESS'S OWN ROOT. The identifier is the
// route's own (OperatorCredentialFile.identifier), so the file the unit is handed and the file the
// harness looks up in $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY stay one datum; only the SOURCE moves, from the system
// credstore to a directory beside the instance the witness supplied.
fn witness_supplied_credstore_dir(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  join([instance.provision_parent_root as String, "/witness-credstore"], "")
}

fn witness_supplied_credential_source(instance: HostDashboardInstance, file: OperatorCredentialFile) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([
    witness_supplied_credstore_dir(instance: instance),
    "/",
    file.identifier as String,
    "-",
    instance.instance_id as String,
  ], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE ARM THAT MAKES THE LIVE CREDSTORE A REFUSAL, NOT A SURPRISE. Reads the FINISHED row through the
// production accessor, so a row that reintroduces WorkerDedicatedAccount -- or any future variant that
// derives a credential outside the supplied root -- is refused here, on every host, instead of reaching
// for /etc/credstore and failing inside the unit where the only diagnostic is a status code.
fn witness_supplied_credential_is_confined(scratch_parent: String, instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> Bool {
  fold(
    dispatch_worker_credentials(instance: instance),
    init: true,
    f: (ok, credential) => ok && is_under(text: credential.credstore_path as String, root: scratch_parent),
  )
}

fn witness_scratch_parent_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  mktemp_directory_command(template: witness_scratch_template() as NonEmptyStr)
}

// THE PRINCIPAL, AS THE HOST SAID IT. `uid`/`gid` are what `install -o/-g` and the readback compare; `user` and
// `group` are the names `find -printf '%u %g'` prints, which is how the modes leg checks the group it set
// (GNU find 4.10.0 exits 1 on an unknown GROUP name, so a name the host does not have is a refusal, not a
// zero).
type WitnessSuppliedPrincipal {
  uid: Int
  gid: Int
  user: NonEmptyStr
  group: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE FOUR QUESTIONS, IN FOUR PROCESSES, deliberately: one `id -u -g -n` collapses the answers into a single
// line, and then a missing group name is indistinguishable from a missing uid, which is the conflation that lets
// a witness "pass" on a host that has no such account. The first three are the sealed builders
// `extdeps.tools.id` provides for a CALLING PROCESS's own credentials (no operand: the account database cannot
// say what a running process holds); the fourth is `gunbc`'s own effective-principal read, the same
// `whoami_command` `gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment` asks when it must know which account a transport
// actually reached rather than which account the database names.
fn witness_uid_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  id_uid_command()
}

fn witness_gid_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  id_gid_command()
}

fn witness_group_name_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  id_group_name_command()
}

fn witness_effective_user_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  whoami_command()
}

// THE FOUR, IN ORDER, for the claim that pins that all four are asked.
fn witness_principal_commands() -> List<ArgvCommand> {
  [
    witness_uid_command(),
    witness_gid_command(),
    witness_group_name_command(),
    witness_effective_user_command(),
  ]
}

type PrincipalNumbers {
  uid: Int
  gid: Int
}

// THE PURE DECISION. Nothing here spawns, so the refusal arms are claimable in the hermetic corpus: a witness
// that silently substituted a production uid for a missing one would have to pass this first. `uid == 0` is
// refused for the reason in the header — running as root makes the DAC legs non-discriminating — and a negative
// uid or gid is what `id` prints when the account cannot be resolved, which is the host SAYING it has no such
// principal.
fn witness_principal_from_texts(
  uid_text: String,
  gid_text: String,
  user_text: String,
  group_text: String,
) -> Optional<WitnessSuppliedPrincipal> {
  match parse_principal_numbers(uid_text: uid_text, gid_text: gid_text) {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: numbers } =>
      if trim(s: user_text) == "" || trim(s: group_text) == "" || numbers.uid == 0 {
        Absent
      } else {
        Present {
          value: WitnessSuppliedPrincipal {
            uid: numbers.uid,
            gid: numbers.gid,
            user: trim(s: user_text) as NonEmptyStr,
            group: trim(s: group_text) as NonEmptyStr,
          },
        }
      }
  }
}

fn parse_principal_numbers(uid_text: String, gid_text: String) -> Optional<PrincipalNumbers> {
  match parse_int(s: trim(s: uid_text)) {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: uid } => match parse_int(s: trim(s: gid_text)) {
      Absent => Absent
      Present { value: gid } =>
        if uid < 0 || gid < 0 {
          Absent
        } else {
          Present { value: PrincipalNumbers { uid: uid, gid: gid } }
        }
    }
  }
}

// THE GUARD THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE FIXTURE. A supplied root is refused when it is, or is under, either
// production lab root or the fleet's owned install root, or when it is NOT under the declared scratch prefix.
// Over-approximation is the safe direction: a substring prefix test also refuses `/opt/gunbc-labX`, and a
// witness that has to move its scratch prefix into the model loses nothing, while a witness that slips past
// this guard writes into the live lab root. Every claim that takes a witness root checks it through this one
// decision.
fn witness_supplied_root_is_refused(candidate: String) -> Bool {
  witness_path_is_outside_supplied_tree(candidate: candidate)
    || witness_resolved_root_is_refused(candidate: candidate)
}

// THE LEXICAL HALF, ON ITS OWN, FOR CALLERS THAT HAVE ALREADY RESOLVED WHAT THEY NEEDED TO RESOLVE. The five
// tests are the same five and they live here and nowhere else; the resolution half is the part a caller may
// not duplicate, and the part it may also not need eleven thousand times. A recursive install derives its
// destinations from one source walk under one root whose RESOLVED spelling that root's own admission has
// already judged, so the destinations are judged lexically here and the root carries the resolution -- the
// reasoning, including the one case that makes it sound (no symbolic link below the root), is at
// `witness_tree_install_destinations_are_admitted`. Splitting the two halves is also what lets a caller state
// which of them it has paid for.
fn witness_path_is_outside_supplied_tree(candidate: String) -> Bool {
  witness_path_spelling_is_refused(candidate: candidate)
    || is_under(text: candidate, root: srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent as String)
    || is_under(text: candidate, root: srv1_dashboard_lab_root as String)
    || is_under(text: candidate, root: host_capacity_home_root as String)
    || !is_under(text: candidate, root: witness_scratch_prefix as String)
}

// EVERY DESTINATION THE LOCUS WRITERS ARE ABOUT TO TOUCH, ADMITTED BEFORE ANY OF THEM IS TOUCHED.
// `witness_batch_locus_base` admits the BASE, and that admission was the whole confinement decision this
// fixture made -- but nothing downstream of it was ever admitted, and a base is not a destination. The locus
// dir, its `dag` directory, the receipt, the receipt seal and the installed binary are each derived by
// joining a caller-controlled key under that base, and `mkdir -p`, `write` and `install` happily follow an
// EXISTING symlink at any of those names. So a descendant planted before the witness ran -- the locus
// directory itself, or a component on the way to the binary -- took the fold's write, the receipt and the
// 0755 executable outside the supplied tree while the base guard went on reporting that the base is fine.
// No race and no root-created escape is needed: on a host where the scratch prefix is world-writable, any
// earlier run or other tenant can plant the link, and the witness then performs the write THROUGH it.
//
// The predicate is deliberately the SAME one the base is judged by. `witness_supplied_root_is_refused`
// resolves the pathname with `realpath -m`, which resolves the components that exist and tolerates the
// trailing ones that do not exist yet -- the state at staging time -- so a symlink standing at a destination
// resolves to whatever it points at and is refused by the ancestry test. Judging the base cannot catch it;
// judging the destination can, and every writer now goes through this one interface.
// THE STAGE LINE AND THE REFUSAL CAUSE, AS ONE NAME EACH. The RED claim asserts against these, and the
// fold and the hand stage both emit them; a string literal in each of three places is how the pair drifts.
data witness_locus_destination_refused_stage: NonEmptyStr = "locus-destination-refused:" as NonEmptyStr
data witness_locus_destination_refused_cause: NonEmptyStr = "the locus destination is not admitted: it resolves outside the supplied tree, because a symlink stands at it or at a component on the way to it: " as NonEmptyStr

fn witness_locus_destination_is_admitted(candidate: String) -> Bool {
  !witness_supplied_root_is_refused(candidate: candidate)
}

// THE COMPLETE SET, spelled once. "Which destinations?" must not depend on remembering the writers: adding a
// writer means adding its destination here, and the claims assert against this list rather than a copy of it.
fn witness_locus_destinations(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> List<String> {
  [
    witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
    witness_locus_dag_dir(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
    witness_locus_receipt_path(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
    witness_locus_receipt_seal_path(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
    witness_locus_binary_path(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
  ]
}

// ALL OF THEM OR NONE. A partial pass is not a pass: staging that admits the directory and then installs the
// binary through a link is exactly the failure this exists to stop.
fn witness_locus_destinations_are_admitted(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> Bool {
  fold(
    witness_locus_destinations(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
    init: true,
    f: (acc, destination) => acc && witness_locus_destination_is_admitted(candidate: destination),
  )
}

// THE FOLD'S OWN DESTINATIONS, DERIVED FROM ITS OWN PLAN -- NOT FROM A REMEMBERED LIST.
// `witness_locus_destinations` names the five paths this module writes by hand, and admitting them was the
// first answer and it was not enough: the tree fold (`approval_broker_tree_install` -> `tree_install_in_realm`)
// does NOT write only those five. It takes `source_root` and `release_locus_tree_subdirs` and, for every entry
// `observe_tree_hashes` reports, writes `locus/<subdir>/<rel>` -- directories with `install -d`
// (`tree_realm_directory_write`), files with `install -m` (`tree_realm_file_write`), links with `cp -P`. The
// elevated realm admits EVERYTHING to its writer (`tree_install_realm_admits` returns true for
// `TreeInstallElevated`, host_effect_realize:6935) and the approval-broker principal is root, so the elevated
// `install -d` walks straight through a symlink standing at `<locus>/src` or at any deeper component under
// `<locus>/dag/...` and lays bytes down wherever the link points -- the elevated readback that follows reports
// the tree that was actually installed, so the escape is invisible after the fact. (The side chat reproduced
// this at review 6006308642 with `install -d` and `install` by hand.) The five-path list cannot see this,
// because the escaped path is a fold destination the list never names.
//
// SO ADMIT THE PLAN, NOT THE LIST. This function walks `source_root/<subdir>` for each of
// `release_locus_tree_subdirs` -- the SAME enumeration the fold's observation walks -- maps each reported entry
// to the path the fold will write it at, and admits every one of those destinations, plus the whole
// `<locus>` subtree must be link-free, BEFORE the fold is handed the locus. It is the SAME predicate
// (`witness_tree_install_destinations_are_admitted`) the guest-source plant is admitted by: one admission
// interface, no private second one, so the fold cannot have a guard that is weaker than the plant's.
data witness_locus_fold_plan_refused_cause: NonEmptyStr =
  "the fold's install plan is not admitted: a destination the source walk reports -- a directory or file the elevated install would create, or a component on the way to one -- resolves outside the supplied tree, or a symbolic link stands in the locus subtree it would write into: " as NonEmptyStr

fn witness_locus_fold_plan_destinations_are_admitted(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  locus_key: String,
) -> Bool {
  let locus = witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key)
  let source_root = instance.repo_root as String
  fold(
    release_locus_tree_subdirs,
    init: true,
    f: (acc, subdir) =>
      acc
        && witness_tree_install_destinations_are_admitted(
          source: join([source_root, "/", subdir], ""),
          destination: join([locus, "/", subdir], ""),
          root: locus,
        ),
  )
}

// THE SAME VERDICT FOR A KEY A CALLER SUPPLIES, before a fold is ever asked to create it -- so a caller can
// name what it refused instead of discovering it as a fold that merely did not converge.
fn witness_locus_target_is_admitted(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> Bool {
  witness_locus_destination_is_admitted(
    candidate: witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
  )
}

// THE SPELLING OF A PATH IS NOT ITS LOCATION. `is_under` is a string-prefix test, and a prefix test cannot see
// through `..`: measured at review 5999428189, `GUNBC_WITNESS_SANDBOX_BASE=/tmp/../opt/gunbc-lab` sits under
// `witness_scratch_prefix` by string test and under `srv1_dashboard_lab_root` once the kernel resolves it, so the
// whole guard stayed GREEN while `mktemp -d` created its tree inside the live install root. A spelling carrying a
// navigation component is therefore refused outright, in this pure function, before any process starts and before
// anything is created. Duplicate slashes are NOT refused here — a leading `/` makes one empty component in every
// absolute path — and are left to the resolved arm below, which compares canonical strings rather than spellings.
fn witness_path_spelling_is_refused(candidate: String) -> Bool {
  !starts_with(s: candidate, prefix: "/")
    || any(
      split(s: candidate, delimiter: "/"),
      c => c == "." || c == "..",
    )
}

// RESOLVE, THEN CHECK, IN THAT ORDER — the arm no lexical rule can replace. A symlink admitted under `/tmp`
// whose target is the live install root, or anything outside the resolved scratch prefix, passes every string
// test ever written, because the string genuinely IS under `/tmp`. `realpath -m` is asked BEFORE the create, so
// the path need not exist yet.
//
// NO ANSWER IS A NO. `wet_out` yields the empty string when realpath cannot be spawned or exits nonzero — a host
// without coreutils, an argument the tool will not accept — and a guard that read silence as permission would be a
// hole exactly the size of the error it swallowed. So an empty answer refuses, and the claim
// `the_guard_refuses_a_path_it_cannot_resolve` names that case instead of leaving it to be inferred from a batch
// of unexplained reds.
//
// WHAT `-m` DOES NOT DO, MEASURED HERE SO THAT NO ONE ADDS AN ARM ON A PREMISE THAT IS FALSE. The romantic failure
// cases do not fail: a path whose component is a regular file, and a path routed through a symlink loop, are both
// ANSWERED, exit 0, no error (`realpath -m -- /etc/passwd/foo` prints `/etc/passwd/foo`). That is the specification
// of `-m` — no component need exist, which is what makes it usable BEFORE a create — and it has two consequences
// for this arm. It means the arm's refusal is about the RESOLVED TEXT and its ancestry, never about whether the
// path is a sensible place to write; a base under a regular file is admitted here and fails later, at the `mktemp`
// that cannot create in it, which is a claim going RED for a host reason and not a confinement hole. And it means
// the silence case is not reachable by arranging a bad component: it is reached when the tool produced nothing at
// all, which is the only state in which reading no answer as permission is possible.
// THE RESOLVED SPELLING OF A PATH THAT NEED NOT EXIST YET, or the empty string when the host answered with
// nothing. One helper, one row: this arm and `witness_path_resolves_under` below both need the resolved text of
// a path before it is created, and a second call site spelling the `realpath -m` argv itself would be a second
// authority for what "resolved" means. Silence -- the tool unspawnable, an argument the tool refuses -- is the
// empty string, and every caller here reads that as a refusal rather than as permission. THE CAPTURE IS THE
// PATH-VALUED ONE (`wet_out_verbatim`): realpath(1) prints the resolved text followed by ONE newline, and
// every byte before that newline is the answer -- realpath resolves what exists, and a name legitimately ends
// in a space (path_resolution(7)). `wet_out`'s whitespace trim collapsed `<base> ` onto `<base>`, so the
// sibling of a run base, named the base plus one space, resolved EQUAL to the base and passed the under-base
// admission while the writer still appended to the UNTRIMMED spelling: guard and write differed by exactly
// what the trim erased. A capture whose trailing bytes are content strips terminators, never whitespace.
fn witness_resolved_path(candidate: String) -> CaptureResult {
  wet_out_zero(command: realpath_canonicalize_missing_zero_command(path: candidate))
}

// EITHER SIDE UNANSWERED REFUSES. A `CaptureRefused` on the candidate or on the prefix is a
// refusal of the root -- never a comparison, never a pass: the variants are branched on
// BEFORE `is_under` sees anything, so two failed resolutions can no more grant ancestry than a
// failed reading can grant possession.
fn witness_resolved_root_is_refused(candidate: String) -> Bool {
  let resolved = witness_resolved_path(candidate: candidate)
  let resolved_prefix = witness_resolved_path(candidate: witness_scratch_prefix as String)
  match resolved {
    CaptureRefused { cause: _ } => true
    ObservedPath { path: text } =>
      match resolved_prefix {
        CaptureRefused { cause: _ } => true
        ObservedPath { path: prefix_path } =>
          is_under(text: text, root: srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent as String)
            || is_under(text: text, root: srv1_dashboard_lab_root as String)
            || is_under(text: text, root: host_capacity_home_root as String)
            || !is_under(text: text, root: prefix_path)
      }
  }
}

// A PREFIX TEST, NOT A SUBSTRING TEST, and the direction matters twice over. A substring test would accept
// `/var/tmp/gunbc-wet-witness-forged` as "under /tmp" (it contains "/tmp/"), which is a witness installing
// outside its declared prefix; and it would accept `/opt/gunbc-labX` as refused while accepting a sibling that
// merely shares a prefix with a production root. So: equal, or begins with the root followed by a separator.
fn is_under(text: String, root: String) -> Bool {
  text == root || starts_with(s: text, prefix: join([root, "/"], ""))
}

// A PATH BELONGS TO A RUN WHEN IT RESOLVES UNDER *THIS RUN'S OWN* BASE -- the directory created for this
// batch, not the scratch prefix that every run on the host shares. The distinction is the defect this exists
// for: the stage trail's own guard asked whether the trail lay under `witness_scratch_prefix`, and two runs
// both live under `/tmp`, so a symbolic link planted at run A's trail name pointing into run B's directory
// answered "yes" and run A appended its capability evidence into run B's trail. A prefix test against the
// selected base is not enough either, for the reason the `-m` arm above already states: the link's own text
// is inside the base. So resolve BOTH sides with the same row the rest of this fixture judges by, and compare
// the resolved texts. Either side unanswered refuses -- no answer is not an answer.
fn witness_path_resolves_under(candidate: String, base: String) -> Bool {
  let resolved = witness_resolved_path(candidate: candidate)
  let resolved_base = witness_resolved_path(candidate: base)
  match resolved {
    ObservedPath { path: text } =>
      match resolved_base {
        ObservedPath { path: base_text } => is_under(text: text, root: base_text)
        CaptureRefused { cause: _ } => false
      }
    CaptureRefused { cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// WHAT THE NAME AT A BASE ACTUALLY IS, AND WHETHER THIS RUN CAN EXCLUSIVELY POSSESS IT -- the two readings
// the ancestry guard cannot stand in for. `witness_supplied_root_is_refused` says where a path RESOLVES: a
// symbolic link standing at a base resolves to whatever it points at, and a link aimed at another tenant's
// tree inside `/tmp` resolves inside the declared prefix and passes every ancestry test ever written. Ask the
// two questions directly, through rows the fleet already owns.
//
// THE POSSESSION QUESTION IS `find_untrusted_entries_command`, the same row `gunbc.host.host_effect_realize`
// puts on its dark-install readback legs to ask whether a locus is trustworthy: with `top_only` it tests the
// operand itself (`-maxdepth 0`, and with -P -- find's default -- a starting point is not followed, so a link
// at the base is seen AS a link) and prints it when it is not owned by `owner`, carries a group- or
// other-write bit (`-perm /022`), or is a symbolic link. Empty stdout is the only admitting answer; a nonzero
// exit is an observation failure and refuses.
//
// This is why a prefix test and an ownership read are each necessary and neither is enough. On a
// single-account host `/tmp` is a directory inside the declared prefix AND owned by the very principal
// running the witness -- the reading that refuses it is the write bit: 1777 means any other tenant can create
// `/tmp/gunbc-sandbox-witness-stages.log` first, and whoever creates the trail name owns the capability
// verdict recorded in it. And `stat -c %F` is asked WITHOUT -L and -H, which coreutils stat(1) documents as
// reporting the operand rather than dereferencing it, so a base that is a plain file, or a link, is refused
// as what it is rather than as what it points at. BOTH READINGS MUST ANSWER: a failed observation is not an
// observation of something safe.
fn witness_path_is_run_owned_directory(candidate: String, owner: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  witness_path_is_directory(candidate: candidate)
    && witness_path_is_exclusively_possessed(candidate: candidate, owner: owner)
}

fn witness_path_is_directory(candidate: String) -> Bool {
  let probe = stat_file_type_command(path: candidate)
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(
    program: probe.program.invocation as String,
    arguments: probe.arguments,
  )
  match stat_file_type_reading(success: read.exit_code == 0, output: read.stdout) {
    StatFileTypeRead { kind: kind } => witness_kind_is_directory(kind: kind)
    StatFileTypeUnanswered { cause: _ } => false
  }
}

fn witness_path_is_exclusively_possessed(candidate: String, owner: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  let probe = find_untrusted_entries_command(
    roots: [candidate],
    top_only: true,
    owner: owner as String,
  )
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(
    program: probe.program.invocation as String,
    arguments: probe.arguments,
  )
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } => false
    ShellExited => read.exit_code == 0 && trim(s: read.stdout) == ""
  }
}

fn witness_kind_is_directory(kind: StatFileKind) -> Bool {
  match kind {
    DirectoryKind => true
    RegularFileKind => false
    SymbolicLinkKind => false
    SocketKind => false
    FifoKind => false
    CharacterDeviceKind => false
    BlockDeviceKind => false
    UnknownKind { word: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE ACTUAL DESTINATIONS OF A RECURSIVE INSTALL, READ OFF THE SOURCE AND ADMITTED BEFORE THE FIRST WRITE.
//
// WHY THE FIXED LIST OF DESTINATIONS WAS NOT ENOUGH. `witness_locus_destinations` names the five paths this
// fixture itself writes -- the receipt, the seal, the binary, and the two top-level tree roots -- and the
// gate admitted exactly those. The source-tree fold then copies `dag/` and `src/v2/` INTO them, which
// creates some eleven thousand directories, files and links whose paths no admission ever saw. A
// pre-existing symbolic link at `<locus>/src` pointing outside the scratch tree, or one nested under
// `<locus>/dag`, is not any of the five, so it passed the whole gate; `cp -r` follows a link at the
// destination's own name and descends through one below it, so the fold wrote THROUGH it, and the elevated
// containment readback that catches an escape arrived only after the bytes had landed. Adding `src/v2` to
// the fixed list would have been the same mistake one level down: the paths that matter are the ones the
// fold is about to create, and the only authority for that set is the source tree being copied.
//
// So the destination set is DERIVED -- one walk of the source, through the fleet's own tree-entry row
// (`find -xdev -printf "%P|%y|%m|%u|%g|%l|%s"` in extdeps/tools/findutils.dag: relative path, type letter,
// mode, owner, group, LINK TARGET, size), which is the shape the recursive copy mirrors. Every derived
// destination is then judged by the SAME lexical rule the supplied root is judged by
// (`witness_path_is_outside_supplied_tree`, factored out of `witness_supplied_root_is_refused` so there is
// one home for it and the fold cannot have a private one), and the destination ROOT by the full rule
// including resolution. What makes eleven thousand per-path resolutions unnecessary is the last leg: NO
// SYMBOLIC LINK AT ALL may stand in the supplied tree before the copy -- the same subtree walk the review
// reproduced the escape with, and the same row `gunbc.host.host_effect_realize` puts on its dark-install
// readback legs. Resolution leaves a tree only by following a link; with the root resolved inside the
// supplied tree and no link anywhere below it, no derived destination can resolve outside, and a link
// created DURING the fold arrives only from the source, where the link arm below refuses it. The
// post-fold containment readback stays in place as the after-the-fact arm: what this closes is the no-race
// case, where the link was simply there already.
//
// A SOURCE ENTRY WHOSE NAME CONTAINS THE COLUMN SEPARATOR shifts every field after it, so a line whose
// column count is not the row's refuses the whole install rather than being read on shifted columns: a
// decision about a path the walk never reported. Likewise a source walk that failed, was refused, or
// printed nothing certifies nothing -- an empty tree is not a tree the witness is installing.
data witness_tree_entry_field_count: Int = 7
data witness_tree_entry_relative_path_field: Int = 0
data witness_tree_entry_type_field: Int = 1
data witness_tree_entry_link_target_field: Int = 5
data witness_tree_entry_column: NonEmptyStr = "|"
data witness_tree_type_symlink_letter: NonEmptyStr = "l"

fn witness_tree_entry_lines(root: String) -> List<String> {
  let probe = find_tree_entry_metadata_command(root: root)
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(
    program: probe.program.invocation as String,
    arguments: probe.arguments,
  )
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } => []
    ShellExited =>
      if read.exit_code != 0 {
        []
      } else {
        fold(split(s: read.stdout, delimiter: "\n"), init: [], f: (acc, line) =>
          if line == "" { acc } else { concat(acc, [line]) })
      }
  }
}

fn witness_tree_entry_field(lines: String, index: Int) -> String {
  match list_at_optional(xs: split(s: lines, delimiter: witness_tree_entry_column as String), index: index) {
    Present { value: field } => field
    Absent => ""
  }
}

fn witness_tree_entry_is_well_formed(line: String) -> Bool {
  length(split(s: line, delimiter: witness_tree_entry_column as String)) == witness_tree_entry_field_count
}

fn witness_tree_destination_for(destination: String, line: String) -> String {
  join([destination, "/", witness_tree_entry_field(lines: line, index: witness_tree_entry_relative_path_field)], "")
}

fn witness_tree_destinations_are_admitted(destination: String, lines: List<String>) -> Bool {
  fold(lines, init: true, f: (ok, line) =>
    ok
      && witness_tree_entry_is_well_formed(line: line)
      && !witness_path_is_outside_supplied_tree(candidate: witness_tree_destination_for(
        destination: destination,
        line: line,
      )))
}

// A LINK COMING FROM THE SOURCE IS THE ONE DESTINATION THIS FOLD CANNOT PREDICT BY LOOKING AT PATHS ALONE.
// `cp -r` copies a source symlink AS a link (it does not follow it), so what lands is a name the witness
// admits and a target it may not: an absolute target names a host path directly, and a relative one walks
// out of the directory the link sits in if it carries a `..` component. Both are refused. This is lexical on
// purpose -- the link arrives inert and nothing here opens it -- and it is the same no-link rule the
// destination walk enforces from the other side, so the planted tree carries no symbolic link at all and the
// guest can only read what it actually opens.
// THE TARGET TEST ITSELF, ONE NAMED PREDICATE OVER THE LINK'S OWN TWO FACTS. It is a function and not a
// block inside the fold because the G0 grammar keeps `block_expr` ABOVE `binary_expr` (see
// `v2.std.grammar` / `extdeps.languages.dag`'s precedence note: a block-shaped operand beside a binary
// operator reads as a record literal, so the forms live above the table, not in an operand position): a
// `{ let ... }` written as the right operand of `||` is not a legal operand, and the strict parse answers
// `parse_g0_tokens_remain` at it. The rule is unchanged -- a target must exist, be relative, carry no `..`
// leg, and the joined path must still resolve inside the supplied tree.
fn witness_tree_source_link_is_inside(target: String, destination: String) -> Bool {
  target != ""
    && !starts_with(s: target, prefix: "/")
    && !string_contains(s: target, pattern: "/..")
    && target != ".."
    && !witness_path_is_outside_supplied_tree(candidate: join([destination, "/", target], ""))
}

fn witness_tree_source_links_stay_inside(destination: String, lines: List<String>) -> Bool {
  fold(lines, init: true, f: (ok, line) =>
    ok
      && (witness_tree_entry_field(lines: line, index: witness_tree_entry_type_field)
            != witness_tree_type_symlink_letter as String
        || witness_tree_source_link_is_inside(
          target: witness_tree_entry_field(lines: line, index: witness_tree_entry_link_target_field),
          destination: destination,
        )))
}

// WHAT THE WHOLE-SUBTREE SCAN POSITIVELY OBSERVED about a destination subtree. The distinction IS the fix.
// The Bool this replaces admitted the subtree whenever the recursive listing failed AND a bare existence probe
// merely came back non-zero -- but an inaccessible ancestor makes the listing fail AND the existence probe fail
// for a reason indistinguishable from "the destination exists", and the elevated installer, which may hold
// access this check did not, then wrote through whatever stood there. Admission now proceeds only on positive
// evidence: LinkFree (the listing ran clean over a present subtree and reported no link) or Absent (the walk
// from the filesystem root found its first missing component omitted by a SUCCESSFUL listing of that
// component's parent -- positive evidence, not two probes failing in the same direction). A link found, and
// every case where the scan could not see -- a listing that failed over a destination that exists, an
// unreadable ancestor at ANY depth, a probe that could not start, find's own refusal -- are refused.
type WitnessTreeSubtreeScanOutcome =
  | WitnessTreeSubtreeLinkFree {}
  | WitnessTreeSubtreeAbsent {}
  | WitnessTreeSubtreeLinkFound { links: String }
  | WitnessTreeSubtreeUnobservable { cause: String }

// THE STATE OF ONE PATH COMPONENT, seen ONLY through a successful listing of its parent. This is the
// positive-evidence machinery the absence answer needs, and it exists because the two reads this witness
// used to trust both answer "no" for reasons that are not absence. `test -e` needs SEARCH (x) permission on
// every ancestor, so a sealed grandparent answers it exactly as a missing name does; `realpath -m` does not
// fail on unreadable components at all (realpath(1): -m resolves what exists and appends the rest), so it
// cannot separate them either. A directory LISTING answers: `find DIR -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -printf
// \'%f\0\'` needs only READ (readdir) on the parent, and a name that a successful listing does not report is
// not in that directory -- while a name it DOES report stands there even when a mode without x hides it from
// `test -e`. path_resolution(7) is the upstream that splits read (enumerate) from search (resolve a name)
// permission; this walk is that split made operational.
type WitnessTreeComponentState =
  | WitnessTreeComponentPresent {}
  | WitnessTreeComponentMissing {}
  | WitnessTreeComponentUnobservable { cause: String }

// THE ENTRIES OF A NUL-TERMINATED CHILD-NAME LISTING (`ls -A --zero`), as words. The listing terminates
// every entry INCLUDING the last, so splitting on NUL leaves exactly one trailing empty word to drop -- and
// only that trailing one. No real
// entry name is empty (a zero-character name cannot exist in a directory) and no name contains NUL (the
// kernel admits every byte but `/` and NUL in a name, path_resolution(7)), so the words between terminators
// ARE the entry names, spaces, tabs, and newlines kept AS CONTENT. Nothing here trims: a whitespace-only
// name is an entry, not an absence of one, and a name containing a newline is one entry, not two.
fn witness_listed_child_names(stdout: String) -> List<String> {
  let words = split(s: stdout, delimiter: "\u{0}")
  if words == [] {
    []
  } else {
    list_init(xs: words)
  }
}

// DOES A SUCCESSFUL LISTING OF `parent` NAME `wanted`? The listing is `ls -A --zero` (row law at
// extdeps.tools.gnu_coreutils.ls_child_names_command): it answers from readdir, so a readable-but-not-
// searchable (0444) parent still names its entries, and a listing is not required to stat children. A
// listing that could not start, or that the host refused (exit non-zero -- an unreadable or missing
// directory, an older coreutils that refuses `--zero`, a filesystem whose d_type gap makes ls stat
// children and fail), is UNOBSERVABLE -- never "the name is not there". Membership is EXACT EQUALITY
// against the NUL-terminated entries: `v2` is not satisfied by `av2x`, a name of one space is satisfied
// only by a name of one space, and a name holding a newline is one whole name.
fn witness_tree_parent_listing_state(parent: String, wanted: String) -> WitnessTreeComponentState {
  let probe = ls_child_names_command(path: parent)
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(
    program: probe.program.invocation as String,
    arguments: probe.arguments,
  )
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } =>
      WitnessTreeComponentUnobservable { cause: join(["parent-listing-spawn-refused:", parent]) }
    ShellExited =>
      if read.exit_code != 0 {
        WitnessTreeComponentUnobservable { cause: join(["parent-listing-failed:", parent]) }
      } else if string_list_contains(
        xs: witness_listed_child_names(stdout: read.stdout),
        wanted: wanted,
      ) {
        WitnessTreeComponentPresent {}
      } else {
        WitnessTreeComponentMissing {}
      }
  }
}

// THE SHAPE A SUPPLIED PATH HAS AT THE BOUNDARY: parsed ONCE into its name segments, and nothing else.
// WHY THE WALK CANNOT DO STRING ARITHMETIC AT ALL. std.filesystem.path_parent_directory drops one
// slash-segment -- including an EMPTY one -- while std.filesystem.path_basename skips empty segments: for
// the input "/a/b/" the parent comes back as "/a/b" (the path itself, minus a slash) and the basename as
// "b", so a walk that chained them would list the directory "/a/b" and ask it for a child named "b" while
// asked about "/a/b" itself -- and a directory that does not contain its own name answers "no such child",
// propagating Missing for a path that is there. A supplied base ending in "/" joined with another "/" is
// exactly this input. The helpers\' disagreement is real and is filed as a standing defect; they are not
// edited here (many consumers, gunbc.design_argument::single_authority). So the walk parses the path once
// and every step and join works on the component LIST. Repeated separators collapse -- "/a/b", "/a/b/" and
// "/a//b" are ONE shape with ONE verdict. ".", ".." and empty input are refused rather than folded: a
// navigating segment cannot be canonicalized without resolving links (which is the very question the walk
// answers), and the supplied roots are absolute, so a relative destination under a scan is an ambiguity
// this witness refuses to guess about (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed).
type WitnessPathShape =
  | WitnessPathRoot { }
  | WitnessPathAbsolute { components: List<String> }
  | WitnessPathRefused { cause: String }

fn witness_path_components(path: String) -> WitnessPathShape {
  if path == "" {
    WitnessPathRefused { cause: "empty-path" }
  } else {
    let segments = split(s: path, delimiter: "/")
    match list_head(xs: segments) {
      Absent => WitnessPathRefused { cause: "empty-path" }
      Present { value: head } =>
        if head != "" {
          WitnessPathRefused { cause: "relative-path" }
        } else if string_list_contains(xs: segments, wanted: ".")
          || string_list_contains(xs: segments, wanted: "..") {
          WitnessPathRefused { cause: "navigating-segment" }
        } else {
          let names = segments |> fold(init: [], f: (acc, seg) =>
            if seg == "" { acc } else { list_push(xs: acc, item: seg) })
          if names == [] {
            WitnessPathRoot {}
          } else {
            WitnessPathAbsolute { components: names }
          }
        }
    }
  }
}

// THE ONE SPELLING every command and cause string is handed: rebuilt from the parsed components, so every
// consumer sees the same canonical path for every spelling of the same location.
fn witness_canonical_absolute_path(names: List<String>) -> String {
  join(["/", join(names, "/")], "")
}

// A SECOND LEGITIMATE SPELLING of the same location with doubled separators between every pair of
// components -- "/a//b" beside "/a/b" and "/a/b/". It parses to the SAME component list, so the walk must
// hand it the SAME verdict; an admission that reads one spelling differently admits a location by its
// spelling, which is the failure mode a path cannot be trusted with.
fn witness_doubled_slash_spelling(names: List<String>) -> String {
  join(["/", join(names, "//")], "")
}

// THE CHILD OF AN ALREADY-CANONICAL prefix by one parsed name. "/" + name for the root; prefix + "/" +
// name otherwise -- built from pieces, never by cutting a string back.
fn witness_child_path(prefix: String, name: String) -> String {
  if prefix == "/" {
    join(["/", name], "")
  } else {
    join([prefix, "/", name], "")
  }
}

// THE WALK FROM THE DEEPEST COMPONENT THAT CAN BE POSITIVELY OBSERVED, over the PARSED component list.
// A component is PRESENT only when its parent\'s listing ran clean and named it; MISSING only when its
// parent\'s listing ran clean and omitted it; and UNOBSERVABLE whenever any ancestor\'s listing could not be
// taken -- because the answer for every component below that one is then unknown, and a walk that stopped
// at the first component it could not even try is the conflation this whole pass exists to kill. The walk
// descends: each step judges one parsed name against the listing of the prefix above it, and only a Present
// answer lets the descent continue -- below a component that is not there, nothing is there either, and
// below one that cannot be seen, nothing can be. The convention at the top is that "/" itself is the
// substrate (an environment without it runs nothing); the listing interface cannot ask it, and no
// destination\'s verdict rests on asking it.
fn witness_tree_names_state(names: List<String>, prefix: String) -> WitnessTreeComponentState {
  match list_head(xs: names) {
    Absent => WitnessTreeComponentPresent {}
    Present { value: first } =>
      match witness_tree_parent_listing_state(parent: prefix, wanted: first) {
        WitnessTreeComponentPresent {} =>
          match list_tail(xs: names) {
            Absent => WitnessTreeComponentPresent {}
            Present { value: rest } =>
              witness_tree_names_state(names: rest, prefix: witness_child_path(prefix: prefix, name: first))
          }
        WitnessTreeComponentMissing {} => WitnessTreeComponentMissing {}
        WitnessTreeComponentUnobservable { cause: c } => WitnessTreeComponentUnobservable { cause: c }
      }
  }
}

// THE POSITIVE SIDE OF ABSENCE, read off the walk over the PARSED path. The destination's own component
// state answers the three questions the old pair of probes conflated: MISSING (the first component the walk
// could not find was omitted by a SUCCESSFUL listing of its parent -- the positive evidence that nothing
// stands at the destination) admits; PRESENT (a clean listing named the destination even though the scan
// failed over it -- the listable-but-not-searchable parent, which `test -e` could not see) refuses;
// UNOBSERVABLE (any listing the walk could not take, at any depth) refuses. A path whose SHAPE was refused
// is unobservable too: a destination this witness cannot even name unambiguously is never admitted.
fn witness_tree_destination_absence_is_positive(canonical: String, names: List<String>) -> WitnessTreeSubtreeScanOutcome {
  match witness_tree_names_state(names: names, prefix: "/") {
    WitnessTreeComponentMissing {} => WitnessTreeSubtreeAbsent {}
    WitnessTreeComponentPresent {} =>
      WitnessTreeSubtreeUnobservable {
        cause: join(["listing-named-destination-despite-scan-failure:", canonical])
      }
    WitnessTreeComponentUnobservable { cause: c } =>
      WitnessTreeSubtreeUnobservable { cause: join(["component-walk:", c]) }
  }
}

// AN ESCAPE TARGET IS EMPTY ONLY WHEN THE LISTING ITSELF SUCCEEDS. `wet_out` maps a refused process to "",
// so `exists && ls == ""` passes BLIND when the host refuses to list the directory -- the exact blindness
// the readbacks of the fold control must not have. This asks the listing for its exit code AND counts its
// ENTRIES: zero entries, from a NUL-terminated listing that RAN (exit 0). A whitespace-only name is an
// entry, so a directory holding one entry named " " is NOT provably empty; nothing here trims.
fn witness_directory_listing_is_provably_empty(path: String) -> Bool {
  let probe = ls_child_names_command(path: path)
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(
    program: probe.program.invocation as String,
    arguments: probe.arguments,
  )
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } => false
    ShellExited => read.exit_code == 0 && witness_listed_child_names(stdout: read.stdout) == []
  }
}

// witness_tree_destination_subtree_outcome runs the scan (find -type l over the subtree) and reports
// which outcome the run was. find(1): exit 0 means every directory it walked was readable -- with no link
// lines that is a link-free subtree, and find reports a missing starting point as an error with exit 1, so
// exit 0 never describes a subtree that was not looked at. exit 1 means the walk hit something it could not
// read or a starting point that is not there, which is exactly the answer the probes cannot tell apart --
// so exit 1 goes to the component walk, which admits only what a successful parent listing omitted. exit 2
// (find's own serious error) is unobservable.
fn witness_tree_destination_subtree_outcome(root: String) -> WitnessTreeSubtreeScanOutcome {
  match witness_path_components(path: root) {
    WitnessPathRefused { cause: c } =>
      WitnessTreeSubtreeUnobservable { cause: join(["path-shape:", c]) }
    WitnessPathRoot {} =>
      WitnessTreeSubtreeUnobservable { cause: join(["destination-is-filesystem-root:", root]) }
    WitnessPathAbsolute { components: names } => {
      let canonical = witness_canonical_absolute_path(names: names)
      let probe = find_symlink_targets_command(root: canonical)
      let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(
        program: probe.program.invocation as String,
        arguments: probe.arguments,
      )
      match read.outcome {
        ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } =>
          WitnessTreeSubtreeUnobservable { cause: join(["scan-spawn-refused:", canonical]) }
        ShellExited =>
          if read.exit_code == 0 {
            if trim(s: read.stdout) == "" {
              WitnessTreeSubtreeLinkFree {}
            } else {
              WitnessTreeSubtreeLinkFound { links: trim(s: read.stdout) }
            }
          } else if read.exit_code == 1 {
            witness_tree_destination_absence_is_positive(canonical: canonical, names: names)
          } else {
            WitnessTreeSubtreeUnobservable { cause: join(["listing-refused:", canonical]) }
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE ADMISSION VIEW of that outcome: only LinkFree and Absent admit; LinkFound and Unobservable refuse.
fn witness_tree_destination_subtree_is_link_free(root: String) -> Bool {
  let outcome = witness_tree_destination_subtree_outcome(root: root)
  outcome == WitnessTreeSubtreeLinkFree {} || outcome == WitnessTreeSubtreeAbsent {}
}

// THE OUTCOME'S OWN WORDS, for a claim that must say WHICH answer it got rather than re-deriving it from
// the Bool.
fn witness_tree_destination_subtree_outcome_label(outcome: WitnessTreeSubtreeScanOutcome) -> String {
  match outcome {
    WitnessTreeSubtreeLinkFree {} => "LinkFree"
    WitnessTreeSubtreeAbsent {} => "Absent"
    WitnessTreeSubtreeLinkFound { links: _ } => "LinkFound"
    WitnessTreeSubtreeUnobservable { cause: _ } => "Unobservable"
  }
}

fn witness_tree_install_destinations_are_admitted(source: String, destination: String, root: String) -> Bool {
  let lines = witness_tree_entry_lines(root: source)
  lines != []
    && witness_locus_destination_is_admitted(candidate: destination)
    && witness_tree_destinations_are_admitted(destination: destination, lines: lines)
    && witness_tree_source_links_stay_inside(destination: destination, lines: lines)
    && witness_tree_destination_subtree_is_link_free(root: root)
}

// THE ONE RECURSIVE INSTALL ROUTE INTO A SUPPLIED TREE, AND THE ONLY ONE. The fold used to call
// `shell.Copy.Recursive` itself and the gate ran somewhere else, over a fixed list, which is how the checked
// set and the written set came to disagree. Here the SAME VALUE answers both: the walk's lines are handed to
// the admission, the copy runs only if that decision was yes, and nothing re-derives the set in between --
// the relationship the review asked to keep through the writer interface is kept by there being no second
// derivation to drift.
//
// THE ORDER IS THE DEFECT. `mkdir -p` of the destination's parent goes FIRST AFTER the decision and never
// before it, because `mkdir -p` writes through a symbolic link: with `<locus>/src` already a link at an empty
// directory elsewhere, the mkdir that prepares `<locus>/src/v2` creates `<elsewhere>/v2` -- a mutation
// outside the supplied tree performed by the code that was supposed to precede the copy, with no gate in
// front of it. Admitting the destination covers the parent too, since the destination's admission RESOLVES
// its whole ancestor chain. A caller that wants a subtree planted calls this and folds its own verdict from
// the Bool; a caller that calls `shell.Copy.Recursive` into a supplied root directly has re-opened the hole
// this function exists to close.
// THE FOLD MAKES THE DESTINATION THE SOURCE, NOT A DIRECTORY INSIDE IT. `cp -r SOURCE DESTINATION` is
// shape-dependent: cp(1) says that if DESTINATION is an existing directory, SOURCE is copied INTO it -- so a
// fold onto a destination that a previous plant left behind produced `<repo>/dag/dag/...` and the claim read
// `<repo>/dag/extdeps/tools/findutils.dag` as missing (measured 2026-11-22 on the P1 claim, where the nested
// leg replanted `dag` after removing a link from under it). The fold is the fixture's only route from a source
// tree to a locus tree, and a route whose result depends on what was already there is not an admission, so the
// destination is removed first. The removal is safe for exactly the reason the gate above exists: the same
// decision that admits the fold established that NO SYMBOLIC LINK STANDS in the destination subtree, so the
// recursive delete cannot be pointed outside the supplied tree -- which is why it goes after the admission and
// not before it, and why a destination that is not there yet is left alone rather than deleted.
fn witness_tree_install_into_supplied_tree(
  source: String,
  destination: String,
  parent: String,
  root: String,
) -> Bool {
  witness_tree_install_destinations_are_admitted(source: source, destination: destination, root: root)
    && wet_ok(command: mkdir_parents_command(path: parent))
    && (shell.Exec.RunArgv(program: "test", arguments: ["-e", destination]).exit_code != 0
      || wet_ok(command: rm_recursive_force_command(path: destination)))
    && shell.Copy.Recursive(
      source: source as FilePath,
      destination: destination as FilePath,
    ).success
}

// THE SINGLE-FILE SIBLING, BECAUSE THE MANIFEST IS COPIED BY THE SAME TOOL. `Cargo.toml` arrives through
// `cp` as the trees do, so it is admitted the same way -- its own destination spelling under the same rule,
// and no link standing in the tree it is being written into. Leaving one `cp` outside the writer because it
// copies one file instead of a subtree is how a second, ungated route survives a fix like this one.
fn witness_file_install_into_supplied_tree(source: String, destination: String, root: String) -> Bool {
  witness_locus_destination_is_admitted(candidate: destination)
    && !witness_path_is_outside_supplied_tree(candidate: destination)
    && witness_tree_destination_subtree_is_link_free(root: root)
    && shell.Copy.Recursive(
      source: source as FilePath,
      destination: destination as FilePath,
    ).success
}

// THE INSTANCE UNDER TEST, AT THE ROOT THE WITNESS SUPPLIED. Every field that is a function of the root is
// re-derived by the SAME production helper the production derivation uses (`dashboard_instance_child`), so
// `source`, `bin/gunbc`, `origin.git`, `inbox/source.bundle`, `attempts` and `attempt-state` have the shape the
// lab's have — the leaf names and relative layout are not restated here, which is what makes this row's shape
// evidence about the production rule rather than about a fixture's idea of it. The four fields that DO come
// from the witness are named, with their reason, at the literal.
fn witness_supplied_instance(
  scratch_parent: FilePath,
  repo: FilePath,
  principal: WitnessSuppliedPrincipal,
) -> HostDashboardInstance {
  let lab = srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()
  let parent = scratch_parent as FilePath
  let root = dashboard_instance_child(root: parent, child: srv1_dashboard_lab_id)
  HostDashboardInstance {
    deployment: lab.deployment,
    instance_id: lab.instance_id,
    host_identity: lab.host_identity,
    // SUPPLIED (1 of 4): the root the witness created, not `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent`. This is the
    // field whose production value produced the srv2 reds and would have created the live lab root.
    provision_parent_root: parent,
    instance_root: root,
    local_origin_root: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "origin.git"),
    local_origin_publish_ref: lab.local_origin_publish_ref,
    // SUPPLIED (2 of 4): a throwaway repository under the same scratch tree. The production value is the lab's
    // live `source` checkout, and these claims drive `git` in this tree — so a production value here is a claim
    // running `git apply` against the lab's checkout.
    repo_root: repo,
    dispatch_base_ref: lab.dispatch_base_ref,
    publication_base_branch: lab.publication_base_branch,
    dispatch_state_scope: lab.dispatch_state_scope,
    // Re-derived, not inherited: `lab.execution_environment` carries the lab's `serve_binary` under
    // `/opt/gunbc-lab`, and a claim that execs the instance's serve binary would then exec the live host's
    // served artifact instead of the one it installed.
    execution_environment: dashboard_execution_environment(
      serve_binary: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    ),
    provider_state: lab.provider_state,
    serve_binary: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    serve_function: lab.serve_function,
    listen_host: lab.listen_host,
    listen_port: lab.listen_port,
    tmux_server: lab.tmux_server,
    actuation: lab.actuation,
    // SUPPLIED (3 of 4): the witness IS its provision target. Inheriting the lab's value here
    // (`DashboardSshProvision { host: "srv1" }`) would have a local test reach into the live host to provision
    // it, which is the defect this row is about in its most literal form.
    actuator_host: lab.host_identity,
    // SUPPLIED (4 of 4): the throwaway repository already exists and holds the anchor, so the seed is
    // "existing repo" rather than the lab's staged bundle. The bundle path is derived from the root, so
    // keeping the lab's arm here would name a bundle under the live lab root that the witness could create.
    source_seed: DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: lab.dispatch_base_ref },
    admitted_revision: lab.admitted_revision,
    toolchain: lab.toolchain,
    dispatch_worker_memory_max: lab.dispatch_worker_memory_max,
    compute_build_memory_ceiling: lab.compute_build_memory_ceiling,
    belt_obligation_memory_max: lab.belt_obligation_memory_max,
    belt_reviewer_memory_max: lab.belt_reviewer_memory_max,
    belt_max_concurrent: lab.belt_max_concurrent,
    // THE PRINCIPAL, AS THE HOST SAID IT. `lab.directory_owner` is srv1's service account name read out of the
    // model; passing it through would have the witness assert ownership against an account database only the
    // live host has (and an absent group makes `find -chgrp` exit 1, whose zero-vs-one is not evidence about
    // the boundary). The uid and gid the claims install and read back are the host's own numbers.
    directory_owner: PosixOwnerNames {
      user: principal.user,
      group: principal.group,
    },
    // THE WORKER VARIANT IS NOT THE LAB ROW'S. srv1_lab_dashboard_instance() is
    // WorkerDedicatedAccount, and dispatch_worker_credentials turns that variant into a
    // LoadCredential= whose source is dispatch_worker_credstore_path --
    // systemd_system_credstore_directory ("/etc/credstore", a data constant in
    // extdeps.systemd.unit_file) + <identifier>-<instance id>. A supplied row that kept the variant
    // therefore asked the systemd MANAGER to read a file under /etc/credstore: on srv2, 2026-11-04,
    // that is exactly what killed launch legs of the sandbox witness with unit exit 243 (CREDENTIALS)
    // before the claim's own command ran, and /etc/credstore is a live root the witness has no
    // business touching. A supplied row carries WorkerIsServiceUser -- the variant srv1_live's own
    // fleet rows carry -- and the credential a supplied unit is handed is built under the witness's
    // own root by witness_supplied_credential_source below. Flipping this field back is not a silent
    // escape: witness_supplied_credential_is_confined reads dispatch_worker_credentials over the
    // finished row and refuses a credential whose path is not under the scratch parent.
    worker: WorkerIsServiceUser,
    materialization_store_root: lab.materialization_store_root,
  }
}

// THE ROOTS A CLAIM USING THIS INSTANCE MAY WRITE UNDER. Enumeration, not a wildcard: a claim that ends up
// writing somewhere else is a claim this set does not cover, and the inhabiting claim over the production row
// (`test.claim.roadmap.dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_witness`) is what keeps this set the same SHAPE as
// the production one. `provider_state.root` is deliberately absent — it is the account's own Codex home, the
// lab's value, and no claim in this family writes there; a claim that starts to must join this set and be
// refused by `witness_supplied_root_is_refused` if it points at a production root.
fn witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<FilePath> {
  [
    instance.provision_parent_root,
    instance.instance_root,
    instance.repo_root,
    instance.local_origin_root,
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "source"),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "bin"),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    dashboard_inbox_under(root: instance.instance_root),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "attempts"),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "attempt-state"),
  ]
}

// THE TARGET A CLAIM GETS WHEN ITS ROOT IS REFUSED. A witness has no `raise`; what it has is the target it
// hands the claim. So when a witness row resolves any of its roots to a refused location, the fixture builds
// it under a prefix that cannot be written at all -- `/proc`, which is not under the scratch prefix and so is
// itself refused by the same decision -- and the claim goes RED at its first `git`/`install` instead of
// touching a tree it has no business touching. Refusing by VALUE, at the one place the target is chosen, is
// how a `Bool`-valued claim says "I will not run here".
data witness_refused_scratch_parent: NonEmptyStr = "/proc/gunbc-witness-root-refused" as NonEmptyStr

fn witness_refused_instance(repo: FilePath) -> HostDashboardInstance {
  witness_supplied_instance(
    scratch_parent: witness_refused_scratch_parent as FilePath,
    repo: repo,
    principal: witness_unnameable_principal(),
  )
}

// THE PRINCIPAL THAT CANNOT EXIST, in one place: negative ids and a `?` name, which `install -o/-g` and
// `chgrp` refuse and which no `find -printf '%u %g'` readback can match. A host that cannot say its own
// account, or a witness whose target was refused, gets this -- so a claim passes only on a principal the
// host actually named.
fn witness_unnameable_principal() -> WitnessSuppliedPrincipal {
  WitnessSuppliedPrincipal {
    uid: -1,
    gid: -1,
    user: "?" as NonEmptyStr,
    group: "?" as NonEmptyStr,
  }
}

// EVERY ONE OF THOSE ROOTS IS OFF THE LIVE HOST, and every one of them came from something the WITNESS
// supplied. The claim form of the guard, so the assertion lives with the fixture it guards and a witness cannot
// opt out of it by forgetting to check.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT THE CLAIM'S OTHER ARM IN DISGUISE. The inhabiting claim's supplied arm compares each root
// against the SCRATCH PREFIX — a corpus data row (`witness_scratch_prefix`, "/tmp"), which any path under /tmp
// satisfies. This one compares each root against the two things THIS ROW was built from: its own
// `instance_root` and its own `repo_root`. Those are different questions, and only this one catches the failure
// mode the witness is actually exposed to — a row whose one child kept a production path while the rest moved,
// or a root handed in from some third directory that happens to sit under /tmp. A prefix test asks "is this in
// the temp area"; this asks "did the witness supply it", which is the property the repair is about. And the
// second arm is not the prefix test either: it names the LIVE host, so no scratch-shaped path can satisfy it.
fn witness_supplied_roots_are_off_the_live_host(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> Bool {
  all(
    xs: witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance),
    predicate: x =>
      is_under(text: x as String, root: instance.instance_root as String)
        || x == (instance.provision_parent_root as String)
        || is_under(text: x as String, root: instance.repo_root as String),
  )
    && all(
      xs: witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance),
      predicate: x => !witness_supplied_root_is_refused(x as String),
    )
    && !any(
      xs: witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance),
      predicate: x => is_under(text: x as String, root: srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent as String),
    )
    && !any(
      xs: witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance),
      predicate: x => x == (srv1_dashboard_lab_root as String),
    )
}

// THE SAME SET, FOR THE PRODUCTION ROW — used only by the inhabiting claim, which writes nothing.
fn witness_production_writable_roots(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<FilePath> {
  [
    instance.provision_parent_root,
    instance.instance_root,
    instance.repo_root,
    instance.local_origin_root,
    instance.serve_binary,
    dashboard_inbox_under(root: instance.instance_root),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "attempts"),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "attempt-state"),
  ]
}

// ============================= THE CLAIM-LOCAL RELEASE LOCUS =============================
// THE DRAIN DOES NOT RUN FROM A SOURCE COPY, AND srv2 PROVED WHAT THAT COST. Stern's S1 at
// `114bcf31` (2026-11-05) lost all nine root-launch legs one step before any sandbox conjunct: the
// elevated drain asked the real CLI to `run` with GUNBC_WORKSPACE_ROOT pointing at the copied guest
// tree, and the CLI answered `workspace_root: ... names no release locus: missing member
// .gunbc-tree-receipt (the tree receipt the locus install writes last; a locus without one was
// interrupted)`. The CLI is right, and it is the same rule production's own drain honours:
// `gunbc.roadmap.attempt_system_unit_launch::attempt_system_unit_launch_gunbc_argv_at`
// passes `workspace_root: dir` where `dir` is `attempt_system_unit_launcher_dir(instance, revision)`
// = `helper_locus_dir(...)`, never a source copy; and the reader
// (`src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs`'s `spawn_workspace_root_locus`) requires that directory to hold a `dag` DIRECTORY plus a regular
// file named by `gunbc.cli_run_workspace_root_scaffold::release_locus_tree_receipt_name`.
// So the supplied root gets its locus the only honest way: through the fold that installs one,
// `gunbc.host.host_effect_realize::approval_broker_tree_install`. That fold installs
// the spine and ancestor dirs, the tree files at 0644 and the binaries at 0755 out of
// `cargo_default_release_dir`, then reads EVERY path back through `find | sha256sum` on both sides,
// refuses unless the two digests agree, and writes the receipt LAST through
// `gunbc.host.host_effect_realize::approval_broker_tree_receipt_write`. Writing the receipt by hand is
// what gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed calls a fabricated default: the file the CLI trusts would be one the test authored without ever
// installing the tree it certifies, and the "was interrupted" half of the CLI's own error message
// would stop meaning anything.
//
// THE ROUTE IS ELEVATED, AND THAT IS THE RULING, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. Every step of the fold is
// sudo-prefixed (`sudo_elevate_command` inside each `tree_install_*_step`), so installing a locus
// needs elevation. The manager's ruling on 2026-11-05 was to call the route AS-IS rather than thread
// an elevation choice into the broker's privileged install — which authorization pattern performs a
// privileged effect sits under gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection, not in a caller's
// parameter. The consequence is carried in the open: on a host with no sudo the install refuses, and
// every claim that needs the locus refuses with the typed cause `witness_locus_install_refusal_cause`
// names — never green, never silently skipped. srv2 has sudo, so there the locus claims and the
// drain-shape probe go green and the skip-the-install mutant goes RED. What catches the class of
// fault in a container with no elevation is the pure discriminator
// `the_drain_names_the_installed_locus_and_not_the_source_copy` in the witness, which asserts the
// drain's own argv against these accessors and needs no elevation to do it.

// `sudo -n true`, as the host ANSWERS it. THE OUTCOME, NOT THE EXIT CODE: `shell.Exec.RunArgv` raises
// when the program cannot be spawned, and a raised claim neither refuses with a cause nor reaches its
// own cleanup — that is the shape that left srv2 holding /tmp/gunbc-sandbox-* trees at 5a4dd7e3. No
// process is a refusal, exactly as a non-zero exit is.
fn witness_sudo_elevation_available() -> Bool {
  let probe = sudo_elevate_command(command: ["/usr/bin/true"])
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(
    program: probe.program.invocation as String,
    arguments: probe.arguments,
  )
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } => false
    ShellExited => read.exit_code == 0
  }
}

// WHY THE ROOT IS HERE AND NOT WHERE PRODUCTION PUTS IT. `attempt_system_unit_launcher_locus` derives
// its base from `attempt_system_unit_launcher_helpers_root`, a LIVE-HOST path: a supplied-root claim
// that installed there would commit the exact offence this witness exists to retire, and the fixture's
// own off-the-live-host arm would refuse it. So the row keeps production's SHAPE — the same
// `helper_locus_dir` fold, and the step stem read from production's own accessor rather than
// restated — and only the base becomes the claim's instance root.
// THE BATCH ROOT EVERY LEG'S LOCUS IS PLACED UNDER, and it is the SAME for every leg. WHY IT IS NOT
// UNDER THE CLAIM ROOT. It used to be `join([instance.instance_root, "/helper-loci"])`, which is
// off-the-live-host and correctly isolated -- and it made every launching leg pay for the whole install
// fold again, because a locus keyed by (claim root, revision) is a NEW locus for every claim: srv2's S1
// at 833ebe0c (stern) measured one fold per leg, zero reuses, and 27.5M eval steps / 634-865 s wall for
// the legs that run it. The locus is read-only content named by the revision it carries, so the sharing
// is the natural key: one tree per revision per batch, installed once, read by every leg. It is still
// under the fixture's scratch prefix and the fixture's off-the-live-host arm still refuses it.
//
// WHAT MAKES TRUSTING IT HONEST. A path in a scratch directory can be created by anyone, so the cheap
// arm does not read a tree's EXISTENCE as a verdict: the fold writes the digest of its own receipt
// beside it, LAST (`witness_seal_locus_receipt`), and `wet_locus_reads_as_installed` re-digests the
// bytes it is about to trust. A shape check -- a line count, or "lines that look like receipts" --
// would have been satisfied by a stub, by a truncated tree, and by a receipt with one path altered;
// `an_unsealed_or_altered_helper_locus_receipt_is_not_read_as_installed` puts all three in front of it
// and flips the verdict back by resealing, so a reader stuck true cannot pass either. What the seal
// does NOT claim is that each digest matches the file beneath it: the fold's own digest agreement does
// that on the leg that runs it, and every launching leg pays anyway for the claim about CONTENT -- the
// served probe, which asks the installed image to answer its own route.
//
// THE ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE THAT NAMES THE RUN. The S1 script mktemps ONE directory per run and exports
// it to both batches, so legs share a locus within a run while two runs on one host never share -- or
// clear -- each other's tree. A FIXED path here would do exactly that, which is why the base comes
// from the host and is then held to the fixture's OWN refusal: an inadmissible name is not honoured,
// and the instance keeps its own tree instead. `witness_batch_locus_base` is read twice per claim and
// nowhere else.
data witness_batch_locus_env_var: String = "WITNESS_LOCUS_BASE"

fn witness_batch_locus_base(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  match shell.Env.Get(name: witness_batch_locus_env_var as NonEmptyStr).value {
    Present { value: named } =>
      if named == "" || witness_supplied_root_is_refused(candidate: named) {
        instance.instance_root as String
      } else {
        named
      }

    Absent => instance.instance_root as String
  }
}

fn witness_claim_helper_locus(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> HelperLocus {
  HelperLocus {
    base: witness_batch_locus_base(instance: instance),
    owner: approval_broker_helper_root_principal,
    step_stem: attempt_system_unit_launcher_locus(instance: instance).step_stem,
  }
}

// THE ONE WAY ANY CONJUNCT NAMES THE LOCUS. A write target or a run target re-derived from a literal
// here would be a second rule beside production's, and a mutation of the real derivation would then
// still find a hand-written path to pass on.
// WHAT A HELPER LOCUS MUST CARRY, in the names the fold is handed. ONE list, two consumers: the install
// route above passes it to `approval_broker_tree_install`, and `the_plant_leaves_the_executables_the_fold_
// contract_requires` requires a staged file for every name in it. A plant that stages half of it is caught
// by the claim instead of by an `install: cannot stat` on a host that can elevate -- which is how srv2 found
// it (fold-cost-small died at `install: cannot stat '<root>/source/target/release/gunbc'`, 3.1M steps of
// setup before file 0) while no local run could, because the local host has no sudo and the fold refuses on
// the elevation probe BEFORE it ever reaches a stat.
data witness_locus_executables: List<String> = [gunbc_release_bin_name]

// THE BINARY THE FOLD INSTALLS IS READ FROM CARGO'S OWN RELEASE DIR UNDER THE SOURCE ROOT
// (`cargo_default_release_dir(build_root: source_root)`, joined with `gunbc_release_bin_name`), so the
// stage is where the fold looks and nowhere else. Staging is a copy of the SAME image the row's serve
// binary is planted from — the claim never gets a locus image it did not itself name.
fn witness_release_stage_dir(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  cargo_default_release_dir(build_root: instance.repo_root as String)
}

fn witness_release_stage_member(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  join([witness_release_stage_dir(instance: instance), "/", gunbc_release_bin_name], "")
}

// STAGE, DO NOT FABRICATE: `image` reaches this only from `GUNBC_WET_SERVE_BINARY` through the fleet's
// one environment read, and the caller has already required it non-empty and executable. Mode 0755 is
// the mode the fold itself installs binaries at, so the staged file and the installed one agree before
// the fold's readback has to agree them.
fn witness_stage_release_binary(instance: HostDashboardInstance, image: String) -> Bool {
  wet_ok(command: mkdir_parents_command(path: witness_release_stage_dir(instance: instance)))
    && wet_ok(command: install_file_mode_command(
      source: image,
      destination: witness_release_stage_member(instance: instance),
      mode: "0755" as NonEmptyStr,
    ))
}

// THE REVISION IS GIT'S ANSWER FOR THE TREE THE LOCUS WILL CARRY, not a string this file invents: the
// guest repo's own HEAD, read with `git rev-parse --verify HEAD` after the plant's commit landed. The
// locus is therefore named for the content it holds — and a plant whose commit never landed cannot name
// one, which is what the empty reading below refuses.
fn witness_guest_tree_revision(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  wet_out(command: git_rev_parse_head_command(repository_dir: instance.repo_root as String))
}

// THE LOCUS IS NAMED BY WHAT THE FOLD INSTALLS, NOT BY THE CLAIM'S COMMIT. srv2 run15794v (2026-11-10)
// measured what the old naming cost: every launching leg plants its own guest tree, so every leg read a
// different HEAD, so every leg named a different locus directory and ran the whole install fold again --
// two digests, two folds, and the second one died inside the interpreter's call-depth ceiling before it
// reached the unit at all. The quantity that IS the same across legs is the content the fold copies: the
// two release subtrees. So the key is production's own digest over those roots -- observe_tree_hashes,
// tree_hash_lines, tree_digest_lines, the same three rows the fold runs to compute the installed_digest
// its receipt attests -- taken over the SOURCE tree, where no elevation is needed and the claim principal
// owns every byte. Two legs whose installed roots hold the same bytes name the SAME locus, so the fold
// runs once per content and every later leg answers with the readback; a leg whose installed roots differ
// by one byte names a different locus and runs the fold.
//
// THE STAGED RELEASE IMAGE IS PART OF WHAT THE LOCUS HOLDS -- the fold's executables step copies it in
// -- so its digest is the key's second component: a leg that staged a different image must not reuse a
// locus holding the old one.
//
// "" means a digest could not be taken, and no leg may paper over that: the caller refuses.
fn witness_locus_installed_roots_digest(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  match observe_tree_hashes(
    root: instance.repo_root as String,
    subs: release_locus_tree_subdirs,
    transport: one_transport_route(transport: LocalShell),
  ) {
    TreeObservationFailed { reason: _ } => ""

    TreeObserved { entries: es } =>
      match tree_digest_lines(
        lines: tree_hash_lines(entries: es),
        transport: one_transport_route(transport: LocalShell),
      ) {
        TreeDigestRefused { reason: _ } => ""

        TreeDigestComputed { hex: h } => h
      }
  }
}

fn witness_locus_key(instance: HostDashboardInstance, image_source: String) -> String {
  let roots = witness_locus_installed_roots_digest(instance: instance)
  let image = witness_file_sha256_hex(path: image_source)

  if roots == "" || image == "" { "" } else { join([roots, "-", image], "") }
}

// THE CAUSE A NO-ELEVATION HOST REPORTS, spelled once so the claim, the probe and the body all name
// the same reason. Chosen so a CI log cannot read it as a skip: it says what is missing and why the
// claim did not proceed.
data witness_locus_needs_elevation_cause: NonEmptyStr =
  "the locus install route needs elevation; this host has no sudo"

// THE SPINE THE FOLD IS TOLD TO OWN, and it is EMPTY. The fold's directory step runs
// `install -d -o <owner> -g <owner> -m 0755` over every spine entry plus the locus (host_effect_realize
// `tree_install_in_realm`: `fold(list_snoc_item(xs: spine, item: locus), ...)`), so a spine entry is a
// directory the fold is told to TAKE OWNERSHIP of, not merely to create.
//
// srv2 run15794r2 (2026-11-09) is what found this. The spine used to name the run-keyed locus base, and
// the first fold on an elevating host therefore re-owned that base to the deploy principal at 0755. Every
// later claim in the same run that creates a NEW locus dir under it as the unprivileged principal then
// gets EACCES: two claims were lost exactly that way, both reading back
// `mkdir -p <base>/<rev>/dag` -> `cannot create directory '<base>/<rev>': Permission denied`
// (`an_unsealed_or_altered_helper_locus_receipt_is_not_read_as_installed`,
// `a_locus_whose_seal_cannot_be_written_is_not_read_as_installed`), and the surviving trail of the
// previous run shows the same interaction one step later — the fold converged and its receipt could not
// be sealed under that base. The base is the HARNESS's directory: the S1 script mktemps it, exports it,
// and other claims write into it. The fold owns the locus it is installing and nothing above it.
//
// THE FILTER IS THE RULE, the empty candidate list is one of its consequences. Any future entry that
// names the run's own base, a claim's own root, or any root the fixture refuses is dropped here, so the
// defect cannot come back as one added line in a list. `the_fold_is_never_handed_the_run_keyed_locus_
// base_as_a_spine_dir` asserts both halves: the spine holds no such entry, AND the filter actually bites
// on the base (a guard that refuses nothing would pass that first half vacuously).
data witness_locus_install_spine_candidates: List<String> = []

fn witness_fold_spine_dir_is_admissible(instance: HostDashboardInstance, candidate: String) -> Bool {
  candidate != witness_batch_locus_base(instance: instance)
    && candidate != (instance.instance_root as String)
    && !witness_supplied_root_is_refused(candidate: candidate)
}

fn witness_locus_install_spine(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<String> {
  filter(
    xs: witness_locus_install_spine_candidates,
    predicate: candidate => witness_fold_spine_dir_is_admissible(instance: instance, candidate: candidate),
  )
}

// PRODUCTION'S FOLD, CALLED AS-IS. The spine is what the fold owns above the locus (see above: nothing,
// by rule); `source_root` is the planted guest tree, whose `dag/` and `src/v2` the fold observes,
// installs and reads back; `executables` is exactly what production's helper-snapshot comment says a
// helper locus holds — `gunbc_release_bin_name` alone.
//
// TWO ARGUMENTS, TWO DIFFERENT JOBS, AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. `locus_key` names the DIRECTORY
// the fold installs into (the digest of what that directory will hold -- see witness_locus_key);
// `revision_text` is the commit that goes in the receipt's `candidate_revision` line, which production
// writes verbatim (release_locus.tree_receipt_body) and the CLI drain reads back. Passing the key where
// the commit belongs would forge a candidate the guest tree never made; passing the commit where the key
// belongs is the naming srv2 run15794v measured as costing a second whole fold per launching leg.
// The refusal arms are the ways this claim cannot proceed -- no content to name a locus by, no commit to
// record, no elevation -- each with its own cause; none is a pass, and the fold's own `TreeInstallRefused`
// names the step that failed.
fn witness_locus_install(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  locus_key: String,
  revision_text: String,
) -> ApprovalBrokerTreeInstallVerdict {
  let revision = RevisionBoundAtEmission { revision: revision_text as CommitSha }
  if locus_key == "" {
    TreeInstallRefused {
      step: TreeStepSourceObserve,
      reason: "the roots the fold would install cannot be digested, so there is no content to name a locus by",
    }
  } else if revision_text == "" {
    TreeInstallRefused {
      step: TreeStepSourceObserve,
      reason: "the guest tree names no commit: the plant's git add/commit did not land, so the fold's receipt could not record a candidate_revision",
    }
  } else if !witness_locus_destinations_are_admitted(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key) {
    // ADMITTED OR REFUSED, BEFORE THE FOLD IS ASKED FOR ANYTHING -- AND BEFORE THE ELEVATION PROBE. The fold
    // creates the locus directory and everything under it from a key this batch chose, so a guard that only
    // ever judged the base never saw the path it was about to populate: an existing symlink at the target
    // turned "install the release locus" into "install the release locus wherever that link points", and the
    // fold reported success. The answer is the same predicate every writer uses -- one verdict -- and it is
    // given BEFORE the effect, so no bytes are ever written through a link this lane could have refused. It
    // is a REFUSAL with its step and its reason, not a silent false, and it carries no capability: nothing
    // about the host was observed. THE ADMISSION IS JUDGED BEFORE THE ELEVATION PROBE ON PURPOSE: a link
    // standing at a destination is a reason to refuse that exists on every host, privileged or not, and a
    // locus that would have been refused for standing on a link must not get to report the missing-sudo
    // excuse instead -- the same ordering lesson the locus-start arm learned, one interface later.
    TreeInstallRefused {
      step: TreeStepInstallDirectory,
      reason: join(
        [witness_locus_destination_refused_cause as String, witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key)],
        "",
      ),
    }
  } else if !witness_locus_fold_plan_destinations_are_admitted(
    instance: instance,
    locus_key: locus_key,
  ) {
    // THE FOLD WRITES MORE THAN THE FIVE, AND ITS DESTINATIONS ARE ADMITTED FROM ITS OWN PLAN. See
    // `witness_locus_fold_plan_destinations_are_admitted`: the elevated `install -d` and `install -m` walk
    // `<locus>/src`, `<locus>/dag/...` and every entry the source observation reports; the elevated realm
    // admits its writer unconditionally (`tree_install_realm_admits` answers true for `TreeInstallElevated`),
    // so a pre-existing symbolic link at any of those paths was written through and the elevated readback
    // certified the escaped tree as installed. The five-path list cannot name those destinations, so the
    // PLAN is admitted instead -- the same source-walk predicate the guest plant is admitted by, over the
    // same fold, judged here before the fold runs and before the elevation probe.
    TreeInstallRefused {
      step: TreeStepInstallDirectory,
      reason: join(
        [witness_locus_fold_plan_refused_cause as String, witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key)],
        "",
      ),
    }
  } else if !witness_sudo_elevation_available() {
    TreeInstallRefused {
      step: TreeStepInstallDirectory,
      reason: witness_locus_needs_elevation_cause as String,
    }
  } else {
    // THE LOCUS RECORD, MINTED HERE AND NOT THROUGH THE TWO SANCTIONED FOLDS, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
    // `release_locus_record` and `helper_release_locus_record` both DERIVE the directory from the
    // revision they carry (`release_locus_dir` / `helper_locus_dir`), which is the right rule for a
    // host release install. This witness is not one: its directory is named by the DIGEST OF WHAT IT
    // WILL HOLD (`witness_locus_key`), while the revision it records is the guest tree's commit — the
    // two arguments do different jobs, spelled above. Routing through either fold would install into
    // the commit-named directory and read the receipt back from the content-named one, which is the
    // split `gunbc.live_deploy.release_locus` exists to remove and srv2 run15794v measured as costing
    // a second whole fold per launching leg. The record is therefore constructed with the directory
    // the fold writes into AND the revision the receipt names, as one value, at one place — and
    // `witness_locus_receipt_path` derives its readback from the SAME `witness_locus_dir_text`, so no
    // caller can carry a directory that drifted from the revision beside it.
    approval_broker_tree_install(
      owner: approval_broker_helper_root_principal,
      locus: ReleaseLocus {
        revision: revision,
        spine: witness_locus_install_spine(instance: instance),
        locus: witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
      },
      source_root: instance.repo_root as String,
      executables: witness_locus_executables,
      // THE EMPTY SIBLING-WRITER FOOTPRINT. `permitted_extras` (added at main by #16927's readback) is
      // the modeled set of members a leg OTHER than the tree install drops at the locus, which the
      // containment readback tolerates by name and refuses everything else. The witness locus has no
      // sibling writers: the tree install writes it and the installer's own receipt is its -- the seal
      // file lands later, by a different route, after this readback has answered -- so the footprint is
      // the empty list, exactly as the helper snapshot and the staged baked release declare.
      permitted_extras: [],
      transport: one_transport_route(transport: LocalShell),
    )
  }
}

// DID THE FOLD CONVERGE — the same question the receipt-last ordering answers, spelled once so no
// claim has to match the production sum to ask it.
fn witness_locus_install_converged(verdict: ApprovalBrokerTreeInstallVerdict) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    TreeInstallConverged { installed_digest: _, files_installed: _ } => true
    TreeInstallRefused { step: _, reason: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE CAUSE, OUT OF THE VERDICT — so a claim's log line says which arm refused instead of leaving a
// bare `false` behind a 245-second leg.
fn witness_locus_install_refusal_cause(verdict: ApprovalBrokerTreeInstallVerdict) -> String {
  match verdict {
    TreeInstallConverged { installed_digest: _, files_installed: _ } => ""
    TreeInstallRefused { step: step, reason: reason } => join([tree_install_step_name(step: step), ": ", reason], "")
  }
}

// THE THREE PATHS THE CLI ASKS ABOUT, ALL OFF THE ONE ACCESSOR. The fold's readback already proved the
// digests agree; these are the paths the drain, the probe and the presence conjuncts all read, so no
// conjunct re-derives a locus path from a literal and a mutation of the real derivation has nowhere
// left to hide. `dir_text` hangs the key under the batch locus base -- the same base
// `witness_claim_helper_locus` names, and the same join production's `helper_locus_dir` performs -- with
// the CONTENT digest in the slot production fills with a candidate sha, because a witness locus is named
// by what it holds and a leg's commit is not that content (see witness_locus_key).
fn witness_locus_dir_text(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> String {
  join([witness_batch_locus_base(instance: instance), "/", locus_key], "")
}

// The `dag` DIRECTORY the reader requires (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs::spawn_workspace_root_locus), inside the locus.
fn witness_locus_dag_dir(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> String {
  join([witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key), "/", "dag"], "")
}

// THE SEAL THE FOLD WRITES LAST: the digest of the receipt's own bytes, in a file beside it. Written
// after the receipt -- which production itself writes last -- so a receipt truncated after the fold
// finished, or edited afterwards, no longer matches what sits beside it.
data witness_locus_receipt_seal_suffix: String = ".sha256"

fn witness_locus_receipt_seal_path(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> String {
  join([witness_locus_receipt_path(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key), witness_locus_receipt_seal_suffix], "")
}

// THE DIGEST OF ONE FILE, through the fleet's own sha256sum route; "" when it cannot be taken.
fn witness_file_sha256_hex(path: String) -> String {
  match sha256sum_line_digest(line: wet_out(command: sha256sum_file_command(path: path))) {
    Absent => ""

    Present { value: digest } => digest.hex as String
  }
}

// EVERY WRITE INTO A LOCUS TREE GOES THROUGH HERE, because the fold installs that tree as the ROOT
// principal (`approval_broker_helper_root_principal`, above) and an unprivileged `Filesystem.Write` into a
// root-owned directory fails. srv2 measured exactly that: three launch legs refused with "the install fold
// converged but its receipt could not be sealed", and the freshness arm failed the same way one step later --
// by then the same revision's locus existed root-owned in the run-keyed base, so `mkdir -p` no-opped and the
// hand stage's own receipt write died, which is what `fresh(b1)=1` actually was. No sudo-less host can see
// this: the fold refuses on the elevation probe before installing anything, so the unprivileged arm below is
// precisely the one the local batch exercises, and the local batch has been green for days on a route srv2
// cannot reach.
//
// FIVE CONSUMERS, ONE BOUNDARY (the seal, the three receipt arms of
// `an_unsealed_or_altered_helper_locus_receipt_is_not_read_as_installed`, and the hand stage), so ownership
// and write route cannot drift apart again. THE VERDICT IS THE READBACK, never the exit code: the bytes must
// come back identical, elevated or not, or the caller is told it failed.
fn witness_write_locus_file(instance: HostDashboardInstance, destination: String, content: String, stage_key: String) -> Bool {
  // THE WRITER JUDGES ITS OWN DESTINATION. Admission used to live only at the base, so any caller could hand
  // this function a pathname and it would write through whatever stood there; the check now travels with the
  // interface, which is the only way the decision taken about the base can still be true of the bytes being
  // written. A refused destination writes NOTHING -- neither at the path it was handed nor at its staging
  // file -- and answers false with no capability marker, because nothing about the host was observed here.
  if !witness_locus_destination_is_admitted(candidate: destination) {
    false
  } else {
  // THE STAGING FILE IS A DESTINATION TOO. It sits under the admitted instance root by CONSTRUCTION, which is
  // exactly the shape the base guard could never see: a link planted earlier at this very name takes the bytes
  // outside the supplied tree while every guard above it keeps reporting the base is fine. So it is admitted
  // here, by the same predicate and before `Filesystem.Write` runs -- and it is admitted on both routes, not
  // only the elevated one that creates it, because which route runs is a fact about the host and this is the
  // one place that decides what these bytes may touch.
  let stage = join([instance.instance_root as String, "/.gunbc-locus-write-", stage_key], "")
  if !witness_locus_destination_is_admitted(candidate: stage) {
    false
  } else {
  let written = if witness_sudo_elevation_available() {
    // Stage where the claim principal can write (its own root, off the live host by the supplied-root guard),
    // then copy it in through the SAME elevated route the install used, at the mode the fold installs at.
    Filesystem.Write(path: stage, content: content).success
      && wet_argv_ok(
        program: sudo_elevate_command(command: ["/usr/bin/install", "-m", "0444", "--", stage, destination]).program.invocation as NonEmptyStr,
        arguments: sudo_elevate_command(command: ["/usr/bin/install", "-m", "0444", "--", stage, destination]).arguments,
      )
  } else {
    Filesystem.Write(path: destination, content: content).success
  }
  let back = Filesystem.Read(path: destination)
  written && back.success && back.content == content
  }
}
  }

// THE SAME BOUNDARY FOR A FILE THAT ALREADY EXISTS ON DISK rather than bytes in hand: the hand stage puts the
// locus binary in with `install_file_mode_command`, which is unprivileged and therefore fails on a locus the
// fold made root-owned. Elevated install, mode preserved, readback by DIGEST -- the bytes are an image, not
// text, and a truncated copy must not pass.
fn witness_install_locus_file(instance: HostDashboardInstance, source: String, destination: String, mode: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  // THE SAME RULE APPLIES TO THE EXECUTABLE, and it matters most here: this is the 0755 binary the drain
  // execs, so an unadmitted destination is not a stray file but an attacker-chosen path receiving an
  // executable the batch then runs. Refused means no `install` is attempted at all, and no capability is
  // claimed -- the host was not consulted on this path.
  if !witness_locus_destination_is_admitted(candidate: destination) {
    false
  } else {
  if !witness_sudo_elevation_available() {
    wet_ok(command: install_file_mode_command(source: source, destination: destination, mode: mode))
      && wet_argv_ok(program: "test" as NonEmptyStr, arguments: ["-e", destination])
  } else {
    wet_argv_ok(
      program: sudo_elevate_command(command: ["/usr/bin/install", "-m", mode as String, "--", source, destination]).program.invocation as NonEmptyStr,
      arguments: sudo_elevate_command(command: ["/usr/bin/install", "-m", mode as String, "--", source, destination]).arguments,
    )
      && wet_argv_ok(program: "test" as NonEmptyStr, arguments: ["-e", destination])
      && witness_file_sha256_hex(path: source) == witness_file_sha256_hex(path: destination)
  }
}
  }

// SEAL IT. A fold that cannot seal its receipt has not delivered a locus the batch may reuse.
fn witness_seal_locus_receipt(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> Bool {
  let hex = witness_file_sha256_hex(path: witness_locus_receipt_path(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key))

  if hex == "" {
    false
  } else {
    witness_write_locus_file(
      instance: instance,
      destination: witness_locus_receipt_seal_path(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
      content: join([hex, "\n"], ""),
      stage_key: join(["seal-", locus_key], ""),
    )
  }
}

// The receipt FILE the reader requires, named by production's constant and no other spelling.
fn witness_locus_receipt_path(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> String {
  join(
    [
      witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
      "/",
      release_locus_tree_receipt_name,
    ],
    "",
  )
}

// The binary the drain execs and the probe execs, named by `gunbc_release_bin_name` — the same name the
// fold installed it under, so the drain cannot quietly begin running the row's serve binary instead.
fn witness_locus_binary_path(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> String {
  join(
    [
      witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key),
      "/",
      gunbc_release_bin_name,
    ],
    "",
  )
}

// BOTH SOURCE ROOTS THE DRAIN PASSES, as production's argv builder spells them: each entry of
// `release_locus_tree_subdirs` joined under the locus dir. A drain that named the source copy here
// would fail the reader for the same reason srv2's did.
fn witness_locus_source_roots(instance: HostDashboardInstance, locus_key: String) -> List<String> {
  map(
    release_locus_tree_subdirs,
    sub => join([witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, locus_key: locus_key), "/", sub], ""),
  )
}
